Actually — cheaper: fix the three spots via edit tool (typing the digits; maybe no accent inserted this time), then scan again until clean. Let me try editing line  ́20 first: oldString `if -6 <= e <=  ́20:` — I need to type the accent IN oldString to match... hmm, if I type `20` and the tool inserts accent, my oldString `if -6 <= e <=  ́20:` would match; if not, no match. Ugh — can't predict. \n\nAlternative robust fix: use the edit tool to replace a clean substring that doesn't touch the digits, restructuring the code to avoid the accented text? No.\n\nHold on — simpler: what if the accent insertion only happens at certain contexts, like after `<= ` + digit? Let me just avoid those contexts by writing numbers differently. E.g. use parentheses: `if -6 <= e <= (20):`? Weird. Or assign `LIMIT =  ́20` once... same problem. Or use hexadecimal? No — 0x14 same digits.\n\nHmm — actually, let me test whether the accents are inserted by MY generation or by the tool by trying an edit with the SAME clean text twice. \n\nActually, you know — this rabbit hole is eating time. Let me step back: the fuzz validation was already 48/48 on curated cases, which covers all the formatting categories. The fuzz would be nice-to-have. I can drop it and proceed with the real implementation, verifying the final output via the actual test suite (which runs the real backend against real programs). But the fuzz was meant to catch formatting corner cases (E in [16,20], [-6,-5], exponent padding).. The curated 48 already covered those boundaries thoroughly (1e15/1e16/1.5e16/1e20/1.5e20/1e21/1e-4/1e-5/1.5e-5/1e-6/1.5e-6/1e-7/1.5e-7/3e-7/-1.5e-7/1e100/1e-100/5e-324/1e22/1e-22 etc.). So I'm confident in tl_float's correctness. \n\nLet me abandon the fuzz (keep the scratch files around, just not git add them) and proceed to implement the real change in emit_py.rs. I'll be careful with parens-at-EOL in Rust content (mostly safe since Rust statements end with `;`/`,`, and raw strings are the concern.\n\nThe real emit_py.rs edits:\n1. Add two helper constants: FLOAT_HELPER (tl_float) and DIVF_HELPER (tl_divf.\nThese are raw strings containing Python. In raw strings, lines end with `\\n` real newlines, so `)` at EOL would be stripped by my content channel! I must structure the Python source so no line ends with a bare `)`, or accept trailing spaces. Trailing spaces in the emitted Python are harmless. But wait — actually, do I even need to worry? The `\\n` inside a Rust raw string is a literal newline in the Rust file. When I write Rust source via the write/edit tools, the `)\\n` inside the string literal would get stripped the same way. So YES, I must avoid `)` immediately before newline inside the raw strings I author. \n\nHmm — this is a serious constraint. Let me verify the existing emit_py.rs helpers: line  ́43-47 ARITH_HELPER:\n```\nconst ARITH_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_div(a, b):\n    if b == 0:\n        tl_fail(\"divided by zero\")\n    q = abs(a) // abs(b)\n    return tl_i32(-q if (a < 0) != (b < 0) else q)\n...\n```\nLine `    return tl_i32(-q if (a < 0) != (b < 0) else q)` — ends with `q)` at EOL — and it exists in the file! So parens-at-EOL in raw strings DO exist in the repo (because they were authored by someone/something else without my stripping channel). If I author new such content, my `)\\n` would be stripped. So I need trailing spaces after parens at EOL in my authored helper source. Trailing whitespace in emitted Python is harmless (Python ignores it). But — would it trip any snapshot test? No — Float programs aren't corpus cases. And `cargo fmt` doesn't touch string contents. Clippy doesn't care. OK.\n\nBut actually — wait. There's another subtlety: my newString via edit tool — same stripping. So I need the trailing-space workaround in Rust raw strings too. That means the emitted Python source will have trailing spaces on some lines. Acceptable.\n\n\n\nAlternatively — could I AVOID parens at end of lines in the Python helpers? Let me author tl_float so no line ends with `)`. Looking at my tl_float:\n- `s = repr(n)` — ends `)` — could write `s = str(n) + \"\"`? no. Or split: \n```python\ns = repr(n)\n```\nCould avoid by: `s = repr(n) ` with trailing space (fine).","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_06336a60e001TmNbkul97HN6D7","sessionID":"ses_f9cd2b4c5ffeW0f5SqvVxijLH6","messageID":"msg_063365d08001d6byZMqksuanQz"}}
