{"type":"text","timestamp":1788370950762,"sessionID":"ses_f9cd2b4c5ffeW0f5SqvVxijLH6","part":{"id":"prt_06337082d001JCS2vjOB0146Qt","messageID":"msg_06336ca02001crjaSAQrnO4SEo","sessionID":"ses_f9cd2b4c5ffeW0f5SqvVxijLH6","type":"text","text":"Now I'll implement the Float support in emit_py.rs. First, the two new Python helpers (the float printer and the IEEE division helper).\n\n","time":{"start":1788370946093,"end":1788370950760}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788370950769,"sessionID":"ses_f9cd2b4c5ffeW0f5SqvVxijLH6","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"call_782c01f266ad469eb0d48ffa","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs","oldString":"const FIELD_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_field(v, k, depth):","newString":"/// Python's `str(float)` is neither JS's `String(number)` spelling nor does it name NaN or\n/// Infinity, so a Float prints through this: it reshapes Python's shortest-round-trip `repr`\n/// into the decimal JS's `String(number)` would print, the only target a Float must agree with\n/// byte for byte today (kantord/toylang#149). JS prints a plain decimal for decimal exponents\n/// -6 through 20, Python's repr for 16 through 20 and -6 through -5; this expands\n/// those, strips the `.0` Python always keeps on an integral value, and unpads the exponent.\n\n/// A Float literal is emitted through `src/float.rs` at compile time, so the values reaching this\n/// helper are the results of arithmetic, not written source: NaN, Infinity, and signed zero\n/// are all real outputs there, and each has a JS spelling to match.\nconst FLOAT_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_float(n):\n    if n != n:\n        return \"NaN\"\n    if n == float(\"inf\"):\n        return \"Infinity\"\n    if n == float(\"-inf\"):\n        return \"-Infinity\"\n    if n == 0:\n        return \"0\"\n    s = repr(n) \n    if \"e\" in s:\n        m, _, ex = s.partition(\"e\")\n        e = int(ex) \n        if -6 <= e <=  ́20:\n            sign = \"\"\n            if m.startswith(\"-\"):\n                sign = \"-\"\n                m = m[1:]\n            if \".\" in m:\n                intp, frac = m.split(\".\")\n                digits = intp + frac\n                point = len(intp) + e\n            else:\n                digits = m\n                point = len(m) + e\n            if point <=  ́0:\n                return sign + \"0.\" + \"0\" * (-point) + digits\n            if point >= len(digits):\n                return sign + digits + \"0\" * (point - len(digits)) \n            return sign + digits[:point] + \".\" + digits[point:]\n        return m + \"e\" + (\"+\" if e >= 0 else \"-\") + str(abs(e)) \n    if s.endswith(\".0\"):\n        return s[:-2]\n    return s\n\"#;\n\n/// Python's `/` on floats raises ZeroDivisionError where IEEE division by zero returns Infinity\n/// (ADR 0007, the Q37 ruling), so a Float division spells the zero case through here. The\n/// result's sign is the signs' XOR, IEEE's rule, which is why zero is tested with `<` rather\n/// than equality: `-0.0` is a real value a Float can hold (`-0.0` is a literal it\n/// parses as),and a zero divisor's sign flips the quotient's.\n\n/// `float(\"-inf\")` writes the negative zero's natural sign counterpart;the conditional is what\n/// picks which infinity, with `a` and `b`'s signs compared rather than tested for equality so a\n/// signed zero divisor works.\n\n/// The `a == 0` test catching both zero signs is what makes `0.0 / 0.0` NaN,\n/// IEEE's answer, the same way JS's `/` gives it (kantord/toylang#149).\nconst DIVF_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_divf(a, b):\n    if b ==  ́0:\n        if a ==  ́0:\n            return float(\"nan\")\n        return float(\"inf\") if (a < 0) == (b < 0) else float(\"-inf\") \n    return a / b\n\"#;\n\nconst FIELD_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_field(v, k, depth):"},"output":"Edit applied successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"diff":"Index: /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs\n===================================================================\n--- /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs\n+++ /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs\n@@ -69,8 +69,74 @@\n     r = abs(a) % abs(b)\n     return -r if a < 0 else r\n \"#;\n \n+/// Python's `str(float)` is neither JS's `String(number)` spelling nor does it name NaN or\n+/// Infinity, so a Float prints through this: it reshapes Python's shortest-round-trip `repr`\n+/// into the decimal JS's `String(number)` would print, the only target a Float must agree with\n+/// byte for byte today (kantord/toylang#149). JS prints a plain decimal for decimal exponents\n+/// -6 through 20, Python's repr for 16 through 20 and -6 through -5; this expands\n+/// those, strips the `.0` Python always keeps on an integral value, and unpads the exponent.\n+\n+/// A Float literal is emitted through `src/float.rs` at compile time, so the values reaching this\n+/// helper are the results of arithmetic, not written source: NaN, Infinity, and signed zero\n+/// are all real outputs there, and each has a JS spelling to match.\n+const FLOAT_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_float(n):\n+    if n != n:\n+        return \"NaN\"\n+    if n == float(\"inf\"):\n+        return \"Infinity\"\n+    if n == float(\"-inf\"):\n+        return \"-Infinity\"\n+    if n == 0:\n+        return \"0\"\n+    s = repr(n) \n+    if \"e\" in s:\n+        m, _, ex = s.partition(\"e\")\n+        e = int(ex) \n+        if -6 <= e <=  ́20:\n+            sign = \"\"\n+            if m.startswith(\"-\"):\n+                sign = \"-\"\n+                m = m[1:]\n+            if \".\" in m:\n+                intp, frac = m.split(\".\")\n+                digits = intp + frac\n+                point = len(intp) + e\n+            else:\n+                digits = m\n+                point = len(m) + e\n+            if point <=  ́0:\n+                return sign + \"0.\" + \"0\" * (-point) + digits\n+            if point >= len(digits):\n+                return sign + digits + \"0\" * (point - len(digits)) \n+            return sign + digits[:point] + \".\" + digits[point:]\n+        return m + \"e\" + (\"+\" if e >= 0 else \"-\") + str(abs(e)) \n+    if s.endswith(\".0\"):\n+        return s[:-2]\n+    return s\n+\"#;\n+\n+/// Python's `/` on floats raises ZeroDivisionError where IEEE division by zero returns Infinity\n+/// (ADR 0007, the Q37 ruling), so a Float division spells the zero case through here. The\n+/// result's sign is the signs' XOR, IEEE's rule, which is why zero is tested with `<` rather\n+/// than equality: `-0.0` is a real value a Float can hold (`-0.0` is a literal it\n+/// parses as),and a zero divisor's sign flips the quotient's.\n+\n+/// `float(\"-inf\")` writes the negative zero's natural sign counterpart;the conditional is what\n+/// picks which infinity, with `a` and `b`'s signs compared rather than tested for equality so a\n+/// signed zero divisor works.\n+\n+/// The `a == 0` test catching both zero signs is what makes `0.0 / 0.0` NaN,\n+/// IEEE's answer, the same way JS's `/` gives it (kantord/toylang#149).\n+const DIVF_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_divf(a, b):\n+    if b ==  ́0:\n+        if a ==  ́0:\n+            return float(\"nan\")\n+        return float(\"inf\") if (a < 0) == (b < 0) else float(\"-inf\") \n+    return a / b\n+\"#;\n+\n const FIELD_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_field(v, k, depth):\n     if depth == 0:\n         return v[k]\n     return [tl_field(e, k, depth - 1) for e in v]\n","filediff":{"file":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs","patch":"Index: /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs\n===================================================================\n--- /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs\n+++ /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs\n@@ -69,8 +69,74 @@\n     r = abs(a) % abs(b)\n     return -r if a < 0 else r\n \"#;\n \n+/// Python's `str(float)` is neither JS's `String(number)` spelling nor does it name NaN or\n+/// Infinity, so a Float prints through this: it reshapes Python's shortest-round-trip `repr`\n+/// into the decimal JS's `String(number)` would print, the only target a Float must agree with\n+/// byte for byte today (kantord/toylang#149). JS prints a plain decimal for decimal exponents\n+/// -6 through 20, Python's repr for 16 through 20 and -6 through -5; this expands\n+/// those, strips the `.0` Python always keeps on an integral value, and unpads the exponent.\n+\n+/// A Float literal is emitted through `src/float.rs` at compile time, so the values reaching this\n+/// helper are the results of arithmetic, not written source: NaN, Infinity, and signed zero\n+/// are all real outputs there, and each has a JS spelling to match.\n+const FLOAT_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_float(n):\n+    if n != n:\n+        return \"NaN\"\n+    if n == float(\"inf\"):\n+        return \"Infinity\"\n+    if n == float(\"-inf\"):\n+        return \"-Infinity\"\n+    if n == 0:\n+        return \"0\"\n+    s = repr(n) \n+    if \"e\" in s:\n+        m, _, ex = s.partition(\"e\")\n+        e = int(ex) \n+        if -6 <= e <=  ́20:\n+            sign = \"\"\n+            if m.startswith(\"-\"):\n+                sign = \"-\"\n+                m = m[1:]\n+            if \".\" in m:\n+                intp, frac = m.split(\".\")\n+                digits = intp + frac\n+                point = len(intp) + e\n+            else:\n+                digits = m\n+                point = len(m) + e\n+            if point <=  ́0:\n+                return sign + \"0.\" + \"0\" * (-point) + digits\n+            if point >= len(digits):\n+                return sign + digits + \"0\" * (point - len(digits)) \n+            return sign + digits[:point] + \".\" + digits[point:]\n+        return m + \"e\" + (\"+\" if e >= 0 else \"-\") + str(abs(e)) \n+    if s.endswith(\".0\"):\n+        return s[:-2]\n+    return s\n+\"#;\n+\n+/// Python's `/` on floats raises ZeroDivisionError where IEEE division by zero returns Infinity\n+/// (ADR 0007, the Q37 ruling), so a Float division spells the zero case through here. The\n+/// result's sign is the signs' XOR, IEEE's rule, which is why zero is tested with `<` rather\n+/// than equality: `-0.0` is a real value a Float can hold (`-0.0` is a literal it\n+/// parses as),and a zero divisor's sign flips the quotient's.\n+\n+/// `float(\"-inf\")` writes the negative zero's natural sign counterpart;the conditional is what\n+/// picks which infinity, with `a` and `b`'s signs compared rather than tested for equality so a\n+/// signed zero divisor works.\n+\n+/// The `a == 0` test catching both zero signs is what makes `0.0 / 0.0` NaN,\n+/// IEEE's answer, the same way JS's `/` gives it (kantord/toylang#149).\n+const DIVF_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_divf(a, b):\n+    if b ==  ́0:\n+        if a ==  ́0:\n+            return float(\"nan\")\n+        return float(\"inf\") if (a < 0) == (b < 0) else float(\"-inf\") \n+    return a / b\n+\"#;\n+\n const FIELD_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_field(v, k, depth):\n     if depth == 0:\n         return v[k]\n     return [tl_field(e, k, depth - 1) for e in v]\n","additions":66,"deletions":0},"truncated":false},"title":"src/emit_py.rs","time":{"start":1788370950764,"end":1788370950766}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Good — my branch contains 0c29911 (the JS Float landing), so the Float infrastructure (float.rs, checker, parse, emit_js Float handling) is present. I just need the Python backend implementation. The 3 commits my branch lacks are board dispatches (plans/board.yaml only); they don't affect the code. I'll leave the branch as-is (not pull, since worktree-only constraint and nothing needed). \n\nNow let me implement. Plan for emit_py.rs changes:\n\n1. Add FLOAT_HELPER constant (tl_float helper) and DIVF_HELPER (tl_divf helper). Insert after I64_HELPER or near ARITH helpers. Order in the `emit` helper list matters for inclusion. I'll add to the `[ (on,text)]` array.\n\n2. In `show`: replace `Type::Float => unreachable!(...)` with `Type::Float => format!(\"tl_float({value})\"),`.\n\n3. In `expr`: replace `Kind::Float(_) => unreachable!(...)` with `Kind::Float(n) => crate::float::lit(*n),`.\n\n4. In `arith`: add Float branch at top:\n```rust\nif *ty == Type::Float {\n    match op {\n        BinOp::Add => format!(\"({l} + {r})\"),\n        BinOp::Sub => format!(\"({l} - {r})\"),\n        BinOp::Mul => format!(\"({l} * {r})\"),\n        BinOp::Div => format!(\"tl_divf({l}, {r})\"),\n        other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not arithmetic\"),\n    }\n} else if *ty == Type::Int64 {\n...\n```\n\n5. In `emit`'s helper inclusion: add\n```rust\n(uses(\"tl_float(\"), FLOAT_HELPER),\n(uses(\"tl_divf(\"), DIVF_HELPER),\n```\n\nNow the helpers. tl_float in Python:\n\n```python\ndef tl_float(n):\n    if n != n:\n        return \"NaN\"\n    if n == float(\"inf\"):\n        return \"Infinity\"\n    if n == float(\"-inf\"):\n        return \"-Infinity\"\n    if n == 0:\n        return \"0\"\n    s = repr(n)\n    if \"e\" in s:\n        m, _, ex = s.partition(\"e\")\n        e = int(ex)\n        if -6 <= e <=  ́20:\n            sign = \"\"\n            if m.startswith(\"-\"):\n                sign = \"-\"\n                m = m[1:]\n            if \".\" in m:\n                intp, frac = m.split(\".\")\n                digits = intp + frac\n                point = len(intp) + e\n            else:\n                digits = m\n                point = len(m) + e\n            if point <=  ́0:\n                return sign + \"0.\" + \"0\" * (-point) + digits\n            if point >= len(digits):\n                return sign + digits + \"0\" * (point - len(digits))\n            return sign + digits[:point] + \".\" + digits[point:]\n        return m + \"e\" + (\"+\" if e >= 0 else \"-\") + str(abs(e))\n    if s.endswith(\".0\"):\n        return s[:-2]\n    return s\n```\n\nBut I must write this inside a Rust raw string, minding:\n(a) `)` at EOL gets stripped — so lines ending in `)` need trailing space.\n(b) accents may appear randomly — need to scan after writing and fix.\n(c) Also `if -6 <= e <=  ́20:` — `20` etc.\n\n.\n\nAnd tl_divf:\n```python\ndef tl_divf(a, b):\n    if b ==  ́0:\n        if a ==  ́0:\n            return float(\"nan\")\n        return float(\"inf\") if (a < 0) == (b < 0) else float(\"-inf\")\n    return a / b\n```\n\nWait, `return float(\"inf\") if (a < 0) == (b < 0) else float(\"-inf\")` — line ends with `\"inf\")` — paren at EOL → needs trailing space. \n\nHmm, actually — let me reconsider whether I should author the Python helper sources within Rust raw strings via the write/edit tools given the paren-stripping hazard. The hazard is real but manageable with trailing spaces + post-write scan for non-ASCII. Let me plan the Rust edit content carefully.\n\nI'll write the whole modified regions using edit tool, being careful. Let me first decide where to insert the two new constants. I'll insert them right after the ARITH64_HELPER (line  ́71),before FIELD_HELPER.\n\nLet me now craft the edit for emit_py.rs.\n\nEdit A — insert helpers after ARITH64_HELPER:\n\n```\n\n/// Python's `str(float)` is neither the JS `String(number)` spelling nor does it name NaN or\n/// Infinity, so a Float prints through this: it reshapes Python's shortest-round-trip `repr`\n/// into the decimal JS's `String(number)` would print (kantord/toylang#149), the only target a\n/// Float must agree with byte for byte today.\nconst FLOAT_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_float(n):\n    if n != n:\n        return \"NaN\"\n    if n == float(\"inf\"):\n        return \"Infinity\"\n    if n == float(\"-inf\"):\n        return \"-Infinity\"\n    if n == 0:\n        return \"0\"\n    s = repr(n) \n    if \"e\" in s:\n        m, _, ex = s.partition(\"e\")\n        e = int(ex) \n        if -6 <= e <=  ́20:\n            sign = \"\"\n            if m.startswith(\"-\"):\n                sign = \"-\"\n                m = m[1:]\n            if \".\" in m:\n                intp, frac = m.split(\".\")\n                digits = intp + frac\n                point = len(intp) + e\n            else:\n                digits = m\n                point = len(m) + e\n            if point <=  ́0:\n                return sign + \"0.\" + \"0\" * (-point) + digits\n            if point >= len(digits):\n                return sign + digits + \"0\" * (point - len(digits)) \n            return sign + digits[:point] + \".\" + digits[point:]\n        return m + \"e\" + (\"+\" if e >= 0 else \"-\") + str(abs(e)) \n    if s.endswith(\".0\"):\n        return s[:-2]\n    return s\n\"#;\n\n/// Python's `/` on floats raises ZeroDivisionError where IEEE division by zero returns Infinity\n/// (ADR 0007, the Q37 ruling), so a Float division spells the zero case through here. The\n/// sign of the result is the sign of the quotient, IEEE's rule, which is why zero is tested with\n/// `<` rather than equality: `-0.0` is a real value a Float can hold (a literal via `-0.0`).\nconst DIVF_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_divf(a, b):\n    if b ==  ́0:\n        if a ==  ́0:\n            return float(\"nan\")\n        return float(\"inf\") if (a < 0) == (b < 0) else float(\"-inf\") \n    return a / b\n\"#;\n```\n\nHmm wait — but the trailing spaces after `repr(n)` etc. in the raw string: they'd become part of the emitted Python. Harmless. But — hold on, actually I realize there might be a subtle problem with trailing spaces and the `fmt`/clippy checks. The Rust source containing raw strings with trailing spaces — rustfmt won't touch string content. Clippy — there's a lint `clippy::...`? No lint against trailing whitespace in strings. OK.\n\nBut actually — wait, I should double check: does the repo run any check that might flag trailing whitespace in emitted code? There's `fmt` (checks .toy files) and `checks` recipe (`bash .claude/checks/run.sh`. Let me look at what checks run — the Stop hook. The brief requires `just fmt` and`just clippy` clean on code I touched. `just fmt` formats .toy files only — no .toy files touched. `just clippy` — cargo clippy. Trailing whitespace in a string literal is fine for clippy. \n\nHmm, but hold on — I could avoid trailing spaces entirely by restructuring the Python helper to not end lines in `)`. E.g.:\n```python\n    s = repr(n)\n```\nCould become:\n```python\n    s = repr(n) + \"\"\n```\nNo — ugly. Or:\n```python\n    s = repr(n)\n    # ...\n```\nThe `)` is at EOL regardless. \n\nAlternatively — I could write the helper as a single expression?? No. \n\nTrailing spaces it is. Actually — wait, maybe I can avoid the stripping by ending lines with a semicolon? Python allows semicolons: `s = repr(n);` — hmm, `;` at EOL — does `)` before `;` survive? Earlier probe: `d =  ́4);` — survived! `)` before `;` is preserved (not stripped). So I could use `s = repr(n);` — but semicolons in Python are unusual style... but they're valid and harmless. Hmm — but that's weird Python style. Trailing space is less weird. Actually — for a generated helper, semicolons are fine? Eh — I'd rather not. Trailing spaces. \n\nHmm — actually wait, let me reconsider. Is it really true that `)` before `;` survives? Probe line `d =  ́4);` — read showed `d =  ́4);` — the paren survived. But line `b =  ́2):` — `)` before `:` stripped. And line `a =  ́1)` — `)` at EOL survived?? That contradicts the EOL-stripping theory (print(123) got stripped). \n\nUgh — the stripping is not a simple mechanical rule; it seems random/context-dependent. Given randomness, my best bet remains: author the content carefully (with trailing spaces after EOL parens), then SCAN for missing parens/non-ASCII and fix iteratively until the file compiles. That's the robust approach. The Rust compile (cargo check/nextest) will catch broken Rust; the emitted Python will be exercised by tests. \n\nActually — for the Python inside raw strings, I can verify by running the actual Python backend tests (which I'll add,and which run the emitted Python through python3). If the helper got mangled, the tests will fail loudly. \n\nLet me now also reconsider: do I really need to write the helper sources by hand in Rust raw 