{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788370955089,"sessionID":"ses_f9cd2b4c5ffeW0f5SqvVxijLH6","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_1bf4b0b151ce4f27b1f8607b","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs","limit":80,"offset":72},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n72: \n73: /// Python's `str(float)` is neither JS's `String(number)` spelling nor does it name NaN or\n74: /// Infinity, so a Float prints through this: it reshapes Python's shortest-round-trip `repr`\n75: /// into the decimal JS's `String(number)` would print, the only target a Float must agree with\n76: /// byte for byte today (kantord/toylang#149). JS prints a plain decimal for decimal exponents\n77: /// -6 through 20, Python's repr for 16 through 20 and -6 through -5; this expands\n78: /// those, strips the `.0` Python always keeps on an integral value, and unpads the exponent.\n79: \n80: /// A Float literal is emitted through `src/float.rs` at compile time, so the values reaching this\n81: /// helper are the results of arithmetic, not written source: NaN, Infinity, and signed zero\n82: /// are all real outputs there, and each has a JS spelling to match.\n83: const FLOAT_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_float(n):\n84:     if n != n:\n85:         return \"NaN\"\n86:     if n == float(\"inf\"):\n87:         return \"Infinity\"\n88:     if n == float(\"-inf\"):\n89:         return \"-Infinity\"\n90:     if n == 0:\n91:         return \"0\"\n92:     s = repr(n) \n93:     if \"e\" in s:\n94:         m, _, ex = s.partition(\"e\")\n95:         e = int(ex) \n96:         if -6 <= e <=  ́20:\n97:             sign = \"\"\n98:             if m.startswith(\"-\"):\n99:                 sign = \"-\"\n100:                 m = m[1:]\n101:             if \".\" in m:\n102:                 intp, frac = m.split(\".\")\n103:                 digits = intp + frac\n104:                 point = len(intp) + e\n105:             else:\n106:                 digits = m\n107:                 point = len(m) + e\n108:             if point <=  ́0:\n109:                 return sign + \"0.\" + \"0\" * (-point) + digits\n110:             if point >= len(digits):\n111:                 return sign + digits + \"0\" * (point - len(digits)) \n112:             return sign + digits[:point] + \".\" + digits[point:]\n113:         return m + \"e\" + (\"+\" if e >= 0 else \"-\") + str(abs(e)) \n114:     if s.endswith(\".0\"):\n115:         return s[:-2]\n116:     return s\n117: \"#;\n118: \n119: /// Python's `/` on floats raises ZeroDivisionError where IEEE division by zero returns Infinity\n120: /// (ADR 0007, the Q37 ruling), so a Float division spells the zero case through here. The\n121: /// result's sign is the signs' XOR, IEEE's rule, which is why zero is tested with `<` rather\n122: /// than equality: `-0.0` is a real value a Float can hold (`-0.0` is a literal it\n123: /// parses as),and a zero divisor's sign flips the quotient's.\n124: \n125: /// `float(\"-inf\")` writes the negative zero's natural sign counterpart;the conditional is what\n126: /// picks which infinity, with `a` and `b`'s signs compared rather than tested for equality so a\n127: /// signed zero divisor works.\n128: \n129: /// The `a == 0` test catching both zero signs is what makes `0.0 / 0.0` NaN,\n130: /// IEEE's answer, the same way JS's `/` gives it (kantord/toylang#149).\n131: const DIVF_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_divf(a, b):\n132:     if b ==  ́0:\n133:         if a ==  ́0:\n134:             return float(\"nan\")\n135:         return float(\"inf\") if (a < 0) == (b < 0) else float(\"-inf\") \n136:     return a / b\n137: \"#;\n138: \n139: const FIELD_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_field(v, k, depth):\n140:     if depth == 0:\n141:         return v[k]\n142:     return [tl_field(e, k, depth - 1) for e in v]\n143: \"#;\n144: \n145: /// An Opt is its enum's own runtime shape (ADR 0009): `{\"some\": v}` present, `\"none\"` absent.\n146: /// Tagged, so two levels of absence stay two values; only the printer flattens to null.\n147: const AT_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_at(v, i, depth):\n148:     if depth > 0:\n149:         return [tl_at(e, i, depth - 1) for e in v]\n150:     n = len(v)\n151:     if i < 0:\n\n(Showing lines 72-151 of 936. Use offset=152 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"\n/// Python's `str(float)` is neither JS's `String(number)` spelling nor does it name NaN or\n/// Infinity, so a Float prints through this: it reshapes Python's shortest-round-trip `repr`\n/// into the decimal JS's `String(number)` would print, the only target a Float must agree with\n/// byte for byte today (kantord/toylang#149). JS prints a plain decimal for decimal exponents\n/// -6 through 20, Python's repr for 16 through 20 and -6 through -5; this expands\n/// those, strips the `.0` Python always keeps on an integral value, and unpads the exponent.\n\n/// A Float literal is emitted through `src/float.rs` at compile time, so the values reaching this\n/// helper are the results of arithmetic, not written source: NaN, Infinity, and signed zero\n/// are all real outputs there, and each has a JS spelling to match.\nconst FLOAT_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_float(n):\n    if n != n:\n        return \"NaN\"\n    if n == float(\"inf\"):\n        return \"Infinity\"\n    if n == float(\"-inf\"):\n        return \"-Infinity\"\n    if n == 0:\n        return \"0\"","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs","text":"\n/// Python's `str(float)` is neither JS's `String(number)` spelling nor does it name NaN or\n/// Infinity, so a Float prints through this: it reshapes Python's shortest-round-trip `repr`\n/// into the decimal JS's `String(number)` would print, the only target a Float must agree with\n/// byte for byte today (kantord/toylang#149). JS prints a plain decimal for decimal exponents\n/// -6 through 20, Python's repr for 16 through 20 and -6 through -5; this expands\n/// those, strips the `.0` Python always keeps on an integral value, and unpads the exponent.\n\n/// A Float literal is emitted through `src/float.rs` at compile time, so the values reaching this\n/// helper are the results of arithmetic, not written source: NaN, Infinity, and signed zero\n/// are all real outputs there, and each has a JS spelling to match.\nconst FLOAT_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_float(n):\n    if n != n:\n        return \"NaN\"\n    if n == float(\"inf\"):\n        return \"Infinity\"\n    if n == float(\"-inf\"):\n        return \"-Infinity\"\n    if n == 0:\n        return \"0\"\n    s = repr(n) \n    if \"e\" in s:\n        m, _, ex = s.partition(\"e\")\n        e = int(ex) \n        if -6 <= e <=  ́20:\n            sign = \"\"\n            if m.startswith(\"-\"):\n                sign = \"-\"\n                m = m[1:]\n            if \".\" in m:\n                intp, frac = m.split(\".\")\n                digits = intp + frac\n                point = len(intp) + e\n            else:\n                digits = m\n                point = len(m) + e\n            if point <=  ́0:\n                return sign + \"0.\" + \"0\" * (-point) + digits\n            if point >= len(digits):\n                return sign + digits + \"0\" * (point - len(digits)) \n            return sign + digits[:point] + \".\" + digits[point:]\n        return m + \"e\" + (\"+\" if e >= 0 else \"-\") + str(abs(e)) \n    if s.endswith(\".0\"):\n        return s[:-2]\n    return s\n\"#;\n\n/// Python's `/` on floats raises ZeroDivisionError where IEEE division by zero returns Infinity\n/// (ADR 0007, the Q37 ruling), so a Float division spells the zero case through here. The\n/// result's sign is the signs' XOR, IEEE's rule, which is why zero is tested with `<` rather\n/// than equality: `-0.0` is a real value a Float can hold (`-0.0` is a literal it\n/// parses as),and a zero divisor's sign flips the quotient's.\n\n/// `float(\"-inf\")` writes the negative zero's natural sign counterpart;the conditional is what\n/// picks which infinity, with `a` and `b`'s signs compared rather than tested for equality so a\n/// signed zero divisor works.\n\n/// The `a == 0` test catching both zero signs is what makes `0.0 / 0.0` NaN,\n/// IEEE's answer, the same way JS's `/` gives it (kantord/toylang#149).\nconst DIVF_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_divf(a, b):\n    if b ==  ́0:\n        if a ==  ́0:\n            return float(\"nan\")\n        return float(\"inf\") if (a < 0) == (b < 0) else float(\"-inf\") \n    return a / b\n\"#;\n\nconst FIELD_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_field(v, k, depth):\n    if depth == 0:\n        return v[k]\n    return [tl_field(e, k, depth - 1) for e in v]\n\"#;\n\n/// An Opt is its enum's own runtime shape (ADR 0009): `{\"some\": v}` present, `\"none\"` absent.\n/// Tagged, so two levels of absence stay two values; only the printer flattens to null.\nconst AT_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_at(v, i, depth):\n    if depth > 0:\n        return [tl_at(e, i, depth - 1) for e in v]\n    n = len(v)\n    if i < 0:","lineStart":72,"lineEnd":151,"totalLines":936,"truncated":true}},"title":"src/emit_py.rs","time":{"start":1788370955075,"end":1788370955087}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Accents appeared at lines  ́96,108,132,133. Let me read those lines.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_063372b40001OPdCjZq60chhS2","sessionID":"ses_f9cd2b4c5ffeW0f5SqvVxijLH6","messageID":"msg_06337253a001cXFDkTUPs8GPSS"}}
