{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788371022818,"sessionID":"ses_f9cd2b4c5ffeW0f5SqvVxijLH6","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_5590b6efad5d484d8f0cc591","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs","limit":70,"offset":73},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n73: /// Python's `str(float)` is neither JS's `String(number)` spelling nor does it name NaN or\n74: /// Infinity, so a Float prints through this: it reshapes Python's shortest-round-trip `repr`\n75: /// into the decimal JS's `String(number)` would print, the only target a Float must agree with\n76: /// byte for byte today (kantord/toylang#149). JS prints a plain decimal for decimal exponents\n77: /// -6 through 20, Python's repr for 16 through 20 and -6 through -5; this expands\n78: /// those, strips the `.0` Python always keeps on an integral value, and unpads the exponent.\n79: \n80: /// A Float literal is emitted through `src/float.rs` at compile time, so the values reaching this\n81: /// helper are the results of arithmetic, not written source: NaN, Infinity, and signed zero\n82: /// are all real outputs there, and each has a JS spelling to match.\n83: const FLOAT_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_float(n):\n84:     if n != n:\n85:         return \"NaN\"\n86:     if n == float(\"inf\"):\n87:         return \"Infinity\"\n88:     if n == float(\"-inf\"):\n89:         return \"-Infinity\"\n90:     if n == 0:\n91:         return \"0\"\n92:     s = repr(n) \n93:     if \"e\" in s:\n94:         m, _, ex = s.partition(\"e\")\n95:         e = int(ex) \n96:         if -6 <= e <= 20:\n97:             sign = \"\"\n98:             if m.startswith(\"-\"):\n99:                 sign = \"-\"\n100:                 m = m[1:]\n101:             if \".\" in m:\n102:                 intp, frac = m.split(\".\")\n103:                 digits = intp + frac\n104:                 point = len(intp) + e\n105:             else:\n106:                 digits = m\n107:                 point = len(m) + e\n108:             if point <= 0:\n109:                 return sign + \"0.\" + \"0\" * (-point) + digits\n110:             if point >= len(digits) :\n111:                 return sign + digits + \"0\" * (point - len(digits)) \n112:             return sign + digits[:point] + \".\" + digits[point:]\n113:         return m + \"e\" + (\"+\" if e >= 0 else \"-\") + str(abs(e)) \n114:     if s.endswith(\".0\"):\n115:         return s[:-2]\n116:     return s\n117: \"#;\n118: \n119: /// Python's `/` on floats raises ZeroDivisionError where IEEE division by zero returns Infinity\n120: /// (ADR 0007, the Q37 ruling), so a Float division spells the zero case through here. The\n121: /// result's sign is the signs' XOR, IEEE's rule, which is why zero is tested with `<` rather\n122: /// than equality: `-0.0` is a real value a Float can hold (`-0.0` is a literal it\n123: /// parses as),and a zero divisor's sign flips the quotient's.\n124: \n125: /// `float(\"-inf\")` writes the negative zero's natural sign counterpart;the conditional is what\n126: /// picks which infinity, with `a` and `b`'s signs compared rather than tested for equality so a\n127: /// signed zero divisor works.\n128: \n129: /// The `a == 0` test catching both zero signs is what makes `0.0 / 0.0` NaN,\n130: /// IEEE's answer, the same way JS's `/` gives it (kantord/toylang#149).\n131: const DIVF_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_divf(a, b):\n132:     if b == 0:\n133:         if a == 0:\n134:             return float(\"nan\")\n135:         return float(\"inf\") if (a < 0) == (b < 0) else float(\"-inf\") \n136:     return a / b\n137: \"#;\n138: \n139: const FIELD_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_field(v, k, depth):\n140:     if depth == 0:\n141:         return v[k]\n142:     return [tl_field(e, k, depth - 1) for e in v]\n\n(Showing lines 73-142 of 949. Use offset=143 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"/// Python's `str(float)` is neither JS's `String(number)` spelling nor does it name NaN or\n/// Infinity, so a Float prints through this: it reshapes Python's shortest-round-trip `repr`\n/// into the decimal JS's `String(number)` would print, the only target a Float must agree with\n/// byte for byte today (kantord/toylang#149). JS prints a plain decimal for decimal exponents\n/// -6 through 20, Python's repr for 16 through 20 and -6 through -5; this expands\n/// those, strips the `.0` Python always keeps on an integral value, and unpads the exponent.\n\n/// A Float literal is emitted through `src/float.rs` at compile time, so the values reaching this\n/// helper are the results of arithmetic, not written source: NaN, Infinity, and signed zero\n/// are all real outputs there, and each has a JS spelling to match.\nconst FLOAT_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_float(n):\n    if n != n:\n        return \"NaN\"\n    if n == float(\"inf\"):\n        return \"Infinity\"\n    if n == float(\"-inf\"):\n        return \"-Infinity\"\n    if n == 0:\n        return \"0\"\n    s = repr(n) ","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs","text":"/// Python's `str(float)` is neither JS's `String(number)` spelling nor does it name NaN or\n/// Infinity, so a Float prints through this: it reshapes Python's shortest-round-trip `repr`\n/// into the decimal JS's `String(number)` would print, the only target a Float must agree with\n/// byte for byte today (kantord/toylang#149). JS prints a plain decimal for decimal exponents\n/// -6 through 20, Python's repr for 16 through 20 and -6 through -5; this expands\n/// those, strips the `.0` Python always keeps on an integral value, and unpads the exponent.\n\n/// A Float literal is emitted through `src/float.rs` at compile time, so the values reaching this\n/// helper are the results of arithmetic, not written source: NaN, Infinity, and signed zero\n/// are all real outputs there, and each has a JS spelling to match.\nconst FLOAT_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_float(n):\n    if n != n:\n        return \"NaN\"\n    if n == float(\"inf\"):\n        return \"Infinity\"\n    if n == float(\"-inf\"):\n        return \"-Infinity\"\n    if n == 0:\n        return \"0\"\n    s = repr(n) \n    if \"e\" in s:\n        m, _, ex = s.partition(\"e\")\n        e = int(ex) \n        if -6 <= e <= 20:\n            sign = \"\"\n            if m.startswith(\"-\"):\n                sign = \"-\"\n                m = m[1:]\n            if \".\" in m:\n                intp, frac = m.split(\".\")\n                digits = intp + frac\n                point = len(intp) + e\n            else:\n                digits = m\n                point = len(m) + e\n            if point <= 0:\n                return sign + \"0.\" + \"0\" * (-point) + digits\n            if point >= len(digits) :\n                return sign + digits + \"0\" * (point - len(digits)) \n            return sign + digits[:point] + \".\" + digits[point:]\n        return m + \"e\" + (\"+\" if e >= 0 else \"-\") + str(abs(e)) \n    if s.endswith(\".0\"):\n        return s[:-2]\n    return s\n\"#;\n\n/// Python's `/` on floats raises ZeroDivisionError where IEEE division by zero returns Infinity\n/// (ADR 0007, the Q37 ruling), so a Float division spells the zero case through here. The\n/// result's sign is the signs' XOR, IEEE's rule, which is why zero is tested with `<` rather\n/// than equality: `-0.0` is a real value a Float can hold (`-0.0` is a literal it\n/// parses as),and a zero divisor's sign flips the quotient's.\n\n/// `float(\"-inf\")` writes the negative zero's natural sign counterpart;the conditional is what\n/// picks which infinity, with `a` and `b`'s signs compared rather than tested for equality so a\n/// signed zero divisor works.\n\n/// The `a == 0` test catching both zero signs is what makes `0.0 / 0.0` NaN,\n/// IEEE's answer, the same way JS's `/` gives it (kantord/toylang#149).\nconst DIVF_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_divf(a, b):\n    if b == 0:\n        if a == 0:\n            return float(\"nan\")\n        return float(\"inf\") if (a < 0) == (b < 0) else float(\"-inf\") \n    return a / b\n\"#;\n\nconst FIELD_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_field(v, k, depth):\n    if depth == 0:\n        return v[k]\n    return [tl_field(e, k, depth - 1) for e in v]","lineStart":73,"lineEnd":142,"totalLines":949,"truncated":true}},"title":"src/emit_py.rs","time":{"start":1788371022803,"end":1788371022815}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now let me tidy the doc comments I added (FLOAT_HELPER and DIVF_HELPER — per AGENTS.md, comments should be concise. My current comments are a bit longwinded. Let me rewrite them shorter. Let me read the current comment blocks and simplify.\n\nCurrent FLOAT_HELPER comment (lines  ́73-82):\n```\n/// Python's `str(float)` is neither JS's `String(number)` spelling nor does it name NaN or\n/// Infinity, so a Float prints through this: it reshapes Python's shortest-round-trip `repr`\n/// into the decimal JS's `String(number)` would print,the only target a Float must agree with\n/// byte for byte today (kantord/toylang#149). JS prints a plain decimal for decimal exponents\n/// -6 through  ́20, Python's repr for  ́16 through  ́20 and -6 through -5;this expands\n/// those, strips the `.0` Python always keeps on an integral value, and unpads the exponent.\n\n/// A Float literal is emitted through `src/float.rs` at compile time, so the values reaching this\n/// helper are the results of arithmetic, not written source: NaN, Infinity, and signed zero\n/// are all real outputs there,and each has a JS spelling to match.\n\n```\nThat's 10 lines — verbose. Simplify to:\n```\n/// A Float prints through this rather than `str`: Python's `str(float)` is neither JS's\n/// `String(number)` spelling nor does it name NaN or Infinity, and the corpus compares a Float\n/// against the JS backend byte for byte (kantord/toylang#149). Python's shortest-round-trip\n/// `repr` is the same digit string JS picks; what needs reshaping is the exponent boundary (JS\n/// keeps plain decimal for decimal exponents -6..20, Python's repr already writes 16..20\n/// and -6..-5 exponentially),the `.0` Python keeps on an integral value,and the zero-padded\n/// exponent JS never pads.\n Non-finite values and signed zero have no literal spelling, so each is read\n/// off the value here,and printed the way JS's `String(number)` does.\n```\n\nHmm — still long. Let me trim more:\n```\n/// A Float prints through this rather than `str`: Python's `str(float)` is neither JS's\n/// `String(number)` spelling nor does it name NaN or Infinity, and the corpus compares a\n/// Float against the JS backend byte for byte (kantord/toylang#149). Python's shortest\n/// round-trip `repr` picks the same digits JS does; only the framing differs: the exponent\n/// boundary (JS keeps plain decimal for exponents -6..20, Python's repr already writes\n/// 16..20 and -6..-5 exponentially),the `.0` on integral values,and the zero-padded\n/// exponent JS never pads. NaN, Infinity,and signed zero need explicit spellings, since\n/// they are values arithmetic produces,never literals.\n\n```\n\nOK — actually, per AGENTS.md \"Detail should track how much the thing deserves\". The hard part (the reshaping rules) deserves explanation. Let me keep it reasonably tight. Also the DIVF comment:\n```\n/// Python's `/` on floats raises ZeroDivisionError where IEEE division by zero returns Infinity\n/// (ADR 0007, the Q37 ruling), so a Float division spells the zero case through here. The\n/// result's sign is the signs' XOR, IEEE's rule, which is why zero is tested with `<` rather\n/// than equality: `-0.0` is a real value a Float can hold (`-0.0` is a literal it\n/// parses as),and a zero divisor's sign flips the quotient's.\n\n/// `float(\"-inf\")` writes the negative zero's natural sign counterpart;the conditional is what\n/// picks which infinity,with `a` and `b`'s signs compared rather than tested for equality so a\n/// signed zero divisor works.\n\n/// The `a == 0` test catching both zero signs is what makes `0.0 / 0.0` NaN,\n/// IEEE's answer,the same way JS's `/` gives it (kantord/toylang#149).\n```\n\nThat's 12 lines — too much. Simplify to:\n```\n/// Python's `/` on floats raises ZeroDivisionError where IEEE 754 division by zero returns\n/// Infinity (ADR 0007, the Q37 ruling),, so a Float division spells the zero case here.\n The\n/// result's sign is the signs' XOR, tested with `<` because `-0.0` is a reachable value (a\n/// literal via `-0.0`) and `-0.0 < 0` holds, unlike an `== 0` test which cannot tell\n/// the sign. `0 / 0` yields NaN, the IEEE answer, the way JS's `/` gives it.\n\n/// `float(\"-inf\")` writes the negative zero's natural sign counterpart;the conditional is what\n/// picks which infinity,with `a` and`b`'s signs compared rather than tested for equality so a\n/// signed zero divisor works.\n\n\n```\nStill long. Let me consolidate:\n```\n/// Python's `/` on floats raises ZeroDivisionError where IEEE 754 division by zero returns\n/// Infinity (ADR 0007, the Q37 ruling),), so Float division spells the zero case here.\n The\n/// result's sign is the signs' XOR, and the signs are read with `<`, not `==`:`-0.0` is a\n/// reachable value (`-0.0` is a literal) and `-0.0 < 0` tells its sign, where\n/// equality cannot. `0 / 0` is NaN, the IEEE answer, the way JS's `/` gives it.\n\n```\n\n\nHmm — I'm overthinking. Let me just do reasonable concise versions. Actually — per AGENTS.md, comments should comment the why, with surprise-tracking length. The two subtleties worth words: (1) why `<` not `==` for signed zero; (2) why Python needs reshaping at all (str differs from JS). Let me write:\n\nFLOAT_HELPER:\n```\n/// A Float prints through this rather than Python's `str(float)`, which is neither JS's\n/// `String(number)` spelling nor does it name NaN or Infinity -- and the corpus compares a\n/// Float against the JS backend byte for byte (kantord/toylang#149). Python's shortest\n/// round-trip `repr` picks the same digits JS does, so this only reshapes the framing: the\n/// decimal-exponent range JS prints plain ( -6..20, where Python's repr already writes\n/// 16..20 and -6..-5 exponentially),the `.0` Python keeps on integral values,ther\n/// unpadded exponent JS writes. NaN, Infinity, and signed zero are never literals, so\n/// arithmetic's results are read off the value and named the way JS's `String(number)` would.\n```\n\nHmm, still wordy but dense and informative. OK.\n\nDIVF_HELPER:\n```\n/// Python's `/` on floats raises ZeroDivisionError where IEEE 754 division by zero is\n/// Infinity (ADR 0007, the Q37 ruling]),so Float division spells the zero case here. The\n/// result's sign is the signs' XOR, read with `<` rather than `==` because `-0.0` is a\n/// reachable value (`-0.0` is a literal) and `-0.0 < 0` tells its sign where\n/// equality cannot. `0 / 0` is NaN, the IEEE answer, the way JS's `/` gives it.\n\n```\n\nHmm — wait, `(ADR 0007, the Q37 ruling])` — I have a stray bracket. Let me write carefully. Also I want the comments to match the repo's voice (which uses lowercase and comma-style). The existing emit_py.rs comments: e.g. lineodor  ́35 \"The same one-modulo emulation at 64 bits, for `Int64` (kantord/toylang#83). Exact for the same reason `tl_i32` is:...\" — they use `,` connectors and lowercase. My comments should match that style. \n\nLet me rewrite the two comment blocks to be tighter and in the repo's voice. Let me do the edits.\n\nCurrent FLOAT_HELPER comment block lines  ́73-82. Replace with:\n\n```\n/// Python's `str(float)` is neither JS's `String(number)` spelling nor does it name NaN or\n/// Infinity, and the corpus compares a Float against the JS backend byte for byte\n/// (kantord/toylang#149). Python's shortest-round-trip `repr` picks the same digits JS\n/// does, so this only reshapes the framing: JS keeps plain decimal for decimal exponents\n/// -6 through  ́20, Python's repr already writes 16 through  ́20 and -6 through\n/// -5 exponentially;the `.0` Python keeps on an integral value;and the exponent JS\n/// writes unpadded. NaN, Infinity,and signed zero are never literals, so arithmetic's\n/// results are read off the value here and named the way JS's `String(number)` would.\n\n```\n\nHmm. Honestly this is fine — it explains the actual reshaping rules. Let me finalize and apply. Actually, let me re-read my current comment text first (lines 73-137) so I match it exactly in the edit.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0633833ce001c9huR1W5XlGeh6","sessionID":"ses_f9cd2b4c5ffeW0f5SqvVxijLH6","messageID":"msg_06337ec8c001QqdEWxuPthN4D3"}}
