{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788371031591,"sessionID":"ses_f9cd2b4c5ffeW0f5SqvVxijLH6","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"call_b05fb1b9d3c44f3f934e0dd4","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs","newString":"/// Python's `str(float)` is neither JS's `String(number)` spelling nor does it name NaN or\n/// Infinity, and the corpus compares a Float against the JS backend byte for byte\n/// (kantord/toylang#149). Python's shortest-round-trip `repr` picks the same digits JS does,\n/// so this only reshapes the framing: JS keeps a plain decimal for decimal exponents -6..20,\n/// Python's repr already writes 16..20 and -6..-5 exponentially;the `.0` Python keeps\n/// on an integral value;and the exponent JS writes unpadded. NaN, Infinity,and signed zero\n/// are never literals, so arithmetic's results are read off the value and named the way JS's\n/// `String(number)` would.","oldString":"/// Python's `str(float)` is neither JS's `String(number)` spelling nor does it name NaN or\n/// Infinity, so a Float prints through this: it reshapes Python's shortest-round-trip `repr`\n/// into the decimal JS's `String(number)` would print, the only target a Float must agree with\n/// byte for byte today (kantord/toylang#149). JS prints a plain decimal for decimal exponents\n/// -6 through 20, Python's repr for 16 through 20 and -6 through -5;this expands\n/// those, strips the `.0` Python always keeps on an integral value, and unpads the exponent.\n\n/// A Float literal is emitted through `src/float.rs` at compile time, so the values reaching this\n/// helper are the results of arithmetic, not written source: NaN, Infinity, and signed zero\n/// are all real outputs there, and each has a JS spelling to match."},"output":"Edit applied successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"diff":"Index: /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs\n===================================================================\n--- /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs\n+++ /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs\n@@ -70,17 +70,15 @@\n     return -r if a < 0 else r\n \"#;\n \n /// Python's `str(float)` is neither JS's `String(number)` spelling nor does it name NaN or\n-/// Infinity, so a Float prints through this: it reshapes Python's shortest-round-trip `repr`\n-/// into the decimal JS's `String(number)` would print, the only target a Float must agree with\n-/// byte for byte today (kantord/toylang#149). JS prints a plain decimal for decimal exponents\n-/// -6 through 20, Python's repr for 16 through 20 and -6 through -5; this expands\n-/// those, strips the `.0` Python always keeps on an integral value, and unpads the exponent.\n-\n-/// A Float literal is emitted through `src/float.rs` at compile time, so the values reaching this\n-/// helper are the results of arithmetic, not written source: NaN, Infinity, and signed zero\n-/// are all real outputs there, and each has a JS spelling to match.\n+/// Infinity, and the corpus compares a Float against the JS backend byte for byte\n+/// (kantord/toylang#149). Python's shortest-round-trip `repr` picks the same digits JS does,\n+/// so this only reshapes the framing: JS keeps a plain decimal for decimal exponents -6..20,\n+/// Python's repr already writes 16..20 and -6..-5 exponentially;the `.0` Python keeps\n+/// on an integral value;and the exponent JS writes unpadded. NaN, Infinity,and signed zero\n+/// are never literals, so arithmetic's results are read off the value and named the way JS's\n+/// `String(number)` would.\n const FLOAT_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_float(n):\n     if n != n:\n         return \"NaN\"\n     if n == float(\"inf\"):\n","filediff":{"file":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs","patch":"Index: /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs\n===================================================================\n--- /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs\n+++ /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs\n@@ -70,17 +70,15 @@\n     return -r if a < 0 else r\n \"#;\n \n /// Python's `str(float)` is neither JS's `String(number)` spelling nor does it name NaN or\n-/// Infinity, so a Float prints through this: it reshapes Python's shortest-round-trip `repr`\n-/// into the decimal JS's `String(number)` would print, the only target a Float must agree with\n-/// byte for byte today (kantord/toylang#149). JS prints a plain decimal for decimal exponents\n-/// -6 through 20, Python's repr for 16 through 20 and -6 through -5; this expands\n-/// those, strips the `.0` Python always keeps on an integral value, and unpads the exponent.\n-\n-/// A Float literal is emitted through `src/float.rs` at compile time, so the values reaching this\n-/// helper are the results of arithmetic, not written source: NaN, Infinity, and signed zero\n-/// are all real outputs there, and each has a JS spelling to match.\n+/// Infinity, and the corpus compares a Float against the JS backend byte for byte\n+/// (kantord/toylang#149). Python's shortest-round-trip `repr` picks the same digits JS does,\n+/// so this only reshapes the framing: JS keeps a plain decimal for decimal exponents -6..20,\n+/// Python's repr already writes 16..20 and -6..-5 exponentially;the `.0` Python keeps\n+/// on an integral value;and the exponent JS writes unpadded. NaN, Infinity,and signed zero\n+/// are never literals, so arithmetic's results are read off the value and named the way JS's\n+/// `String(number)` would.\n const FLOAT_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_float(n):\n     if n != n:\n         return \"NaN\"\n     if n == float(\"inf\"):\n","additions":7,"deletions":9},"truncated":false},"title":"src/emit_py.rs","time":{"start":1788371031585,"end":1788371031588}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me simplify the two comment blocks. I'll replace lines  ́73-82 (FLOAT_HELPER comment) and lines  ́119-130 (DIVF_HELPER comment) with tighter versions.\n\nFLOAT_HELPER comment replacement:\n```\n/// Python's `str(float)` is neither JS's `String(number)` spelling nor does it name NaN or\n/// Infinity, and the corpus compares a Float against the JS backend byte for byte\n/// (kantord/toylang#149). Python's shortest-round-trip `repr` picks the same digits JS does,\n/// so this only reshapes the framing: JS keeps a plain decimal for decimal exponents -6..20,\n/// Python's repr already writes 16..20 and -6..-5 exponentially;the `.0` Python keeps\n/// on an integral value;and the exponent JS writes unpadded. NaN, Infinity,and signed zero\n/// are never literals, so arithmetic's results are read off the value and named the way JS's\n/// `String(number)` would.\n```\n\nDIVF_HELPER comment replacement:\n```\n/// Python's `/` on floats raises ZeroDivisionError where IEEE 754 division by zero returns\n/// Infinity (ADR 0007, the Q37 ruling),), so a Float division spells the zero case\n/// through here. The result's sign is the signs' XOR, IEEE's rule, read with `<` rather than\n/// `==` because `-0.0` is a reachable value (`-0.0` is a literal)and `-0.0 < 0`\n/// tells its sign where equality cannot. `0 / 0` is NaN, the IEEE answer, the way JS's\n/// `/` gives it (kantord/toylang#149).\n```\n\nWait — `(ADR 0007, the Q37 ruling])` — I wrote `ruling],` earlier by accident; here I'll write `ruling)` properly. Let me apply these two edits. The oldString spans lines  ́73-82 and  ́119-130. I'll match the exact current text.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0633855e20010BxSIIxHRB5oKs","sessionID":"ses_f9cd2b4c5ffeW0f5SqvVxijLH6","messageID":"msg_0633833fc001CIC92m0KUeLaFE"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788371031603,"sessionID":"ses_f9cd2b4c5ffeW0f5SqvVxijLH6","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"call_c0f25430da154dd9b165cce1","state":{"status":"error","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs","newString":"/// Python's `/` on floats raises ZeroDivisionError where IEEE 754 division by zero returns\n/// Infinity (ADR 0007, the Q37 ruling),), so a Float division spells the zero case\n/// through here. The result's sign is the signs' XOR, IEEE's rule, read with `<` rather than\n/// `==` because `-0.0` is a reachable value (`-0.0` is a literal)and `-0.0 < 0`\n/// tells its sign where equality cannot. `0 / 0` is NaN, the IEEE answer, the way JS's\n/// `/` gives it (kantord/toylang#149).","oldString":"/// Python's `/` on floats raises ZeroDivisionError where IEEE division by zero returns Infinity\n/// (ADR 0007,ther Q37 ruling[], so a Float division spells the zero case through here. The\n/// result's sign is the signs' XOR, IEEE's rule, which is why zero is tested with `<` rather\n/// than equality: `-0.0` is a real value a Float can hold (`-0.0` is a literal it\n/// parses as),and a zero divisor's sign flips the quotient's.\n\n/// `float(\"-inf\")` writes the negative zero's natural sign counterpart;the conditional is what\n/// picks which infinity,with `a` and `b`'s signs compared rather than tested for equality so a\n/// signed zero divisor works.\n\n/// The `a == 0` test catching both zero signs is what makes `0.0 / 0.0` NaN,\n/// IEEE's answer,ther same way JS's `/` gives it (kantord/toylang#149)."},"error":"Could not find oldString in the file. It must match exactly, including whitespace, indentation, and line endings.","time":{"start":1788371031533,"end":1788371031601}},"id":"prt_0633855e9001vFtYDwzM1N8cVq","sessionID":"ses_f9cd2b4c5ffeW0f5SqvVxijLH6","messageID":"msg_0633833fc001CIC92m0KUeLaFE"}}
