{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788371034619,"sessionID":"ses_f9cd2b4c5ffeW0f5SqvVxijLH6","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_16b3a45acf7242adb38aaf3e","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs","limit":25,"offset":112},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n112:     if s.endswith(\".0\"):\n113:         return s[:-2]\n114:     return s\n115: \"#;\n116: \n117: /// Python's `/` on floats raises ZeroDivisionError where IEEE division by zero returns Infinity\n118: /// (ADR 0007, the Q37 ruling), so a Float division spells the zero case through here. The\n119: /// result's sign is the signs' XOR, IEEE's rule, which is why zero is tested with `<` rather\n120: /// than equality: `-0.0` is a real value a Float can hold (`-0.0` is a literal it\n121: /// parses as),and a zero divisor's sign flips the quotient's.\n122: \n123: /// `float(\"-inf\")` writes the negative zero's natural sign counterpart;the conditional is what\n124: /// picks which infinity, with `a` and `b`'s signs compared rather than tested for equality so a\n125: /// signed zero divisor works.\n126: \n127: /// The `a == 0` test catching both zero signs is what makes `0.0 / 0.0` NaN,\n128: /// IEEE's answer, the same way JS's `/` gives it (kantord/toylang#149).\n129: const DIVF_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_divf(a, b):\n130:     if b == 0:\n131:         if a == 0:\n132:             return float(\"nan\")\n133:         return float(\"inf\") if (a < 0) == (b < 0) else float(\"-inf\") \n134:     return a / b\n135: \"#;\n136: \n\n(Showing lines 112-136 of 947. Use offset=137 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"    if s.endswith(\".0\"):\n        return s[:-2]\n    return s\n\"#;\n\n/// Python's `/` on floats raises ZeroDivisionError where IEEE division by zero returns Infinity\n/// (ADR 0007, the Q37 ruling), so a Float division spells the zero case through here. The\n/// result's sign is the signs' XOR, IEEE's rule, which is why zero is tested with `<` rather\n/// than equality: `-0.0` is a real value a Float can hold (`-0.0` is a literal it\n/// parses as),and a zero divisor's sign flips the quotient's.\n\n/// `float(\"-inf\")` writes the negative zero's natural sign counterpart;the conditional is what\n/// picks which infinity, with `a` and `b`'s signs compared rather than tested for equality so a\n/// signed zero divisor works.\n\n/// The `a == 0` test catching both zero signs is what makes `0.0 / 0.0` NaN,\n/// IEEE's answer, the same way JS's `/` gives it (kantord/toylang#149).\nconst DIVF_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_divf(a, b):\n    if b == 0:\n        if a == 0:","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs","text":"    if s.endswith(\".0\"):\n        return s[:-2]\n    return s\n\"#;\n\n/// Python's `/` on floats raises ZeroDivisionError where IEEE division by zero returns Infinity\n/// (ADR 0007, the Q37 ruling), so a Float division spells the zero case through here. The\n/// result's sign is the signs' XOR, IEEE's rule, which is why zero is tested with `<` rather\n/// than equality: `-0.0` is a real value a Float can hold (`-0.0` is a literal it\n/// parses as),and a zero divisor's sign flips the quotient's.\n\n/// `float(\"-inf\")` writes the negative zero's natural sign counterpart;the conditional is what\n/// picks which infinity, with `a` and `b`'s signs compared rather than tested for equality so a\n/// signed zero divisor works.\n\n/// The `a == 0` test catching both zero signs is what makes `0.0 / 0.0` NaN,\n/// IEEE's answer, the same way JS's `/` gives it (kantord/toylang#149).\nconst DIVF_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_divf(a, b):\n    if b == 0:\n        if a == 0:\n            return float(\"nan\")\n        return float(\"inf\") if (a < 0) == (b < 0) else float(\"-inf\") \n    return a / b\n\"#;\n","lineStart":112,"lineEnd":136,"totalLines":947,"truncated":true}},"title":"src/emit_py.rs","time":{"start":1788371034611,"end":1788371034617}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The second edit failed — probably the oldString has a mismatched char (`ruling[],` vs `ruling`...). Let me re-read the current lines  ́113-135 to get exact text.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0633861f1001jkkzLnDn0h68nr","sessionID":"ses_f9cd2b4c5ffeW0f5SqvVxijLH6","messageID":"msg_063385664001mZFhfowlrKZqbg"}}
