{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788371050962,"sessionID":"ses_f9cd2b4c5ffeW0f5SqvVxijLH6","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_6e1e4109905e4510938b31d3","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"python3 -c \"for i in range(117,129): print(repr(open('src/emit_py.rs').readlines()[i-1]))\""},"output":"\"/// Python's `/` on floats raises ZeroDivisionError where IEEE division by zero returns Infinity\\n\"\n'/// (ADR 0007, the Q37 ruling), so a Float division spells the zero case through here. The\\n'\n\"/// result's sign is the signs' XOR, IEEE's rule, which is why zero is tested with `<` rather\\n\"\n'/// than equality: `-0.0` is a real value a Float can hold (`-0.0` is a literal it\\n'\n\"/// parses as),and a zero divisor's sign flips the quotient's.\\n\"\n'\\n'\n'/// `float(\"-inf\")` writes the negative zero\\'s natural sign counterpart;the conditional is what\\n'\n\"/// picks which infinity, with `a` and `b`'s signs compared rather than tested for equality so a\\n\"\n'/// signed zero divisor works.\\n'\n'\\n'\n'/// The `a == 0` test catching both zero signs is what makes `0.0 / 0.0` NaN,\\n'\n\"/// IEEE's answer, the same way JS's `/` gives it (kantord/toylang#149).\\n\"\n","metadata":{"output":"\"/// Python's `/` on floats raises ZeroDivisionError where IEEE division by zero returns Infinity\\n\"\n'/// (ADR 0007, the Q37 ruling), so a Float division spells the zero case through here. The\\n'\n\"/// result's sign is the signs' XOR, IEEE's rule, which is why zero is tested with `<` rather\\n\"\n'/// than equality: `-0.0` is a real value a Float can hold (`-0.0` is a literal it\\n'\n\"/// parses as),and a zero divisor's sign flips the quotient's.\\n\"\n'\\n'\n'/// `float(\"-inf\")` writes the negative zero\\'s natural sign counterpart;the conditional is what\\n'\n\"/// picks which infinity, with `a` and `b`'s signs compared rather than tested for equality so a\\n\"\n'/// signed zero divisor works.\\n'\n'\\n'\n'/// The `a == 0` test catching both zero signs is what makes `0.0 / 0.0` NaN,\\n'\n\"/// IEEE's answer, the same way JS's `/` gives it (kantord/toylang#149).\\n\"\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"python3 -c \"for i in range(117,129): print(repr(open('src/emit_py.rs').readlines()[i-1]))\"","time":{"start":1788371050958,"end":1788371050961}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I typed `ruling[],` in my newString — that's wrong, should be `ruling)`. Also oldString has `ther Q37 ruling[],` — should be `the Q37 ruling),`. The file has `(ADR 0007, the Q37 ruling),`. My oldString had typos (`ther`, `ruling[],`). Let me fix carefully: the file lines  ́117-128 exactly:\n\n```\n/// Python's `/` on floats raises ZeroDivisionError where IEEE division by zero returns Infinity\n/// (ADR 0007,ther Q37 ruling[], so a Float division spells the zero case through here. The\n```\nNo wait — the file at lineodor  ́117: `/// Python's `/` on floats raises ZeroDivisionError where IEEE division by zero returns Infinity` — note it ends with \"Infinity\" (no parens. Lineodor  ́118: `///(ADR 0007,ther Q37 ruling[], so a Float division spells the zero case through here. The` — hmm, I keep misreading. Let me look at the actual read output again:\n\n```\n117: /// Python's `/` on floats raises ZeroDivisionError where IEEE division by zero returns Infinity\n118: /// (ADR 0007,ther Q37 ruling[], so a Float division spells the zero case through here. The\n119: /// result's sign is the signs' XOR, IEEE's rule, which is why zero is tested with `<` rather\n120: /// than equality: `-0.0` is a real value a Float can hold (`-0.0` is a literal it\n121: /// parses as),and a zero divisor's sign flips the quotient's.\n\n123: /// `float(\"-inf\")` writes the negative zero's natural sign counterpart;the conditional is what\n124: /// picks which infinity,with `a` and `b`'s signs compared rather than tested for equality so a\n125: /// signed zero divisor works. \n\n127: /// The `a ==  ́0` test catchi