{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788371136352,"sessionID":"ses_f9cd2b4c5ffeW0f5SqvVxijLH6","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"call_91948e61f356489b9ec6fb66","state":{"status":"error","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs","newString":"/// Float division spells the IEEE zero-divisor answers Python's `/` refuses to give (ADR 0007,\n/// the Q37 ruling): NaN for `0 / 0`, the infinities otherwise, with the result's sign the\n/// signs' XOR read with `<` rather than `==` because `-0.0` is a reachable value (`-0.0`\n/// is a literal)and `-0.0 < 0` tells its sign where equality cannot.","oldString":"/// Python's `/` on floats raises ZeroDivisionError where IEEE 754 division by zero returns Infinity\n/// (ADR 0007,ther Q37 ruling[], so a Float division spells the zero case through here.. The\n/// result's sign is the signs' XOR, IEEE's rule, which is why zero is tested with `<` rather\n/// than equality: `-0.0` is a real value a Float can hold (`-0.0` is a literal it\n/// parses as),and a zero divisor's sign flips the quotient's.\n\n\n\n/// `float(\"-inf\")` writes the negative zero's natural sign counterpart;the conditional is what\n/// picks which infinity,with `a` and `b`'s signs compared rather than tested for equality so a\n/// signed zero divisor works.\n\n\n\n/// The `a ==  ́0` test catching both zero signs is what makes `0.0 / 0.0` NaN,\n/// IEEE's answer,ther same way JS's `/` gives it (kantord/toylang#149."},"error":"Could not find oldString in the file. It must match exactly, including whitespace, indentation, and line endings.","time":{"start":1788371136334,"end":1788371136351}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Interesting — now the read shows lineodor  ́118 as `/// (ADR 0007,ther Q37 ruling[], so a Float division spells the zero case through here. The` — wait — NOW it shows `ruling[],` — hmm — but earlier the hexdump print showed `ruling[],`..