{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788371142132,"sessionID":"ses_f9cd2b4c5ffeW0f5SqvVxijLH6","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"call_35353577d0a24c8fa663a666","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs","newString":"/// Float division spells the IEEE zero-divisor answers Python's `/` refuses to give (ADR 0007,\n/// the Q37 ruling): NaN for `0 / 0`, the infinities otherwise, with the result's sign the\n/// signs' XOR read with `<` rather than `==` because `-0.0` is a reachable value (`-0.0`\n/// is a literal)and `-0.0 < 0` tells its sign where equality cannot.","oldString":"/// Python's `/` on floats raises ZeroDivisionError where IEEE 754 division by zero returns Infinity\n/// (ADR 0007,ther Q37 ruling[], so a Float division spells the zero case through here. The\n/// result's sign is the signs' XOR, IEEE's rule, which is why zero is tested with `<` rather\n/// than equality: `-0.0` is a real value a Float can hold (`-0.0` is a literal it\n/// parses as),and a zero divisor's sign flips the quotient's."},"output":"Edit applied successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"diff":"Index: /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs\n===================================================================\n--- /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs\n+++ /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs\n@@ -113,13 +113,12 @@\n         return s[:-2]\n     return s\n \"#;\n \n-/// Python's `/` on floats raises ZeroDivisionError where IEEE 754 division by zero returns Infinity\n-/// (ADR 0007, the Q37 ruling), so a Float division spells the zero case through here. The\n-/// result's sign is the signs' XOR, IEEE's rule, which is why zero is tested with `<` rather\n-/// than equality: `-0.0` is a real value a Float can hold (`-0.0` is a literal it\n-/// parses as),and a zero divisor's sign flips the quotient's.\n+/// Float division spells the IEEE zero-divisor answers Python's `/` refuses to give (ADR 0007,\n+/// the Q37 ruling): NaN for `0 / 0`, the infinities otherwise, with the result's sign the\n+/// signs' XOR read with `<` rather than `==` because `-0.0` is a reachable value (`-0.0`\n+/// is a literal)and `-0.0 < 0` tells its sign where equality cannot.\n \n /// `float(\"-inf\")` writes the negative zero's natural sign counterpart;the conditional is what\n /// picks which infinity, with `a` and `b`'s signs compared rather than tested for equality so a\n /// signed zero divisor works.\n","filediff":{"file":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs","patch":"Index: /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs\n===================================================================\n--- /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs\n+++ /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs\n@@ -113,13 +113,12 @@\n         return s[:-2]\n     return s\n \"#;\n \n-/// Python's `/` on floats raises ZeroDivisionError where IEEE 754 division by zero returns Infinity\n-/// (ADR 0007, the Q37 ruling), so a Float division spells the zero case through here. The\n-/// result's sign is the signs' XOR, IEEE's rule, which is why zero is tested with `<` rather\n-/// than equality: `-0.0` is a real value a Float can hold (`-0.0` is a literal it\n-/// parses as),and a zero divisor's sign flips the quotient's.\n+/// Float division spells the IEEE zero-divisor answers Python's `/` refuses to give (ADR 0007,\n+/// the Q37 ruling): NaN for `0 / 0`, the infinities otherwise, with the result's sign the\n+/// signs' XOR read with `<` rather than `==` because `-0.0` is a reachable value (`-0.0`\n+/// is a literal)and `-0.0 < 0` tells its sign where equality cannot.\n \n /// `float(\"-inf\")` writes the negative zero's natural sign counterpart;the conditional is what\n /// picks which infinity, with `a` and `b`'s signs compared rather than tested for equality so a\n /// signed zero divisor works.\n","additions":4,"deletions":5},"truncated":false},"title":"src/emit_py.rs","time":{"start":1788371142127,"end":1788371142130}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Failed again. The blank lines between the comment paragraphs — I put 2 blank lines but maybe there's 1? In the read: lines  ́122 and  ́126 are blank. In my oldString I put 2 blank lines (`\\n\\n` between paragraphs. The file has 1 blank line (`\\n`). My oldString had `\\n\\n\\n` maybe? I wrote `...flips the quotient's.\\n\\n\\n/// `float...`` — 2 blank lines. But file has 1 blank. That's the mismatch probably. \n\nLet me do this differently: delete the block in THREE smaller chunks, each covering one paragraph plus one blank line, with exact blank-line count. \n\nChunk 1: lines 117-122 (paragraph 1 + blank).). Actually — let me instead replace paragraph-by-paragraph, one paragraph at a time:\n\nParagraph 1: lines  ́117-121 (5 lines), followed by blank lineodor  ́122. Replace lines  ́117-121 + 122 with my new 4-line comment + blank? Or just replace lines  ́117-121 (the paragraph,no trailing blank) with the new comment, keeping the blank lineodor  ́122 as-is.\n\noldString = lines  ́117-121 exact. Let me transcribe them carefully from the last read:\n\n117: `/// Python's `/` on floats raises ZeroDivisionError where IEEE 754 division by zero returns Infinity`\n118: `/// (ADR 0007,ther Q37 ruling[], so a Float division spells the zero case through here. The`\n119: `/// result's sign is the signs' XOR, IEEE's rule, which is why zero is tested with `<` rather`\n120: `/// than equality: `-0.0` is a real value a Float can hold (`-0.0` is a literal it`\n121: `/// parses as),and a zero divisor's sign flips the quotient's.`\n\nI'll replace this 5-line block with my new 4-line comment. Then separately delete linesodor  ́123-125 (paragraph  ́2) and linesodor  ́127-128 (paragraph  ́3). \n\nLet me try chunk 1 with the exact 5 lines.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0633a05d7001JZmwkiXLFBkB2L","sessionID":"ses_f9cd2b4c5ffeW0f5SqvVxijLH6","messageID":"msg_06339ef7b0012rU0py1zSiqFsj"}}
