{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788371143764,"sessionID":"ses_f9cd2b4c5ffeW0f5SqvVxijLH6","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_98ab6369afa24162959cc970","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs","limit":18,"offset":115},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n115: \"#;\n116: \n117: /// Float division spells the IEEE zero-divisor answers Python's `/` refuses to give (ADR 0007,\n118: /// the Q37 ruling): NaN for `0 / 0`, the infinities otherwise, with the result's sign the\n119: /// signs' XOR read with `<` rather than `==` because `-0.0` is a reachable value (`-0.0`\n120: /// is a literal)and `-0.0 < 0` tells its sign where equality cannot.\n121: \n122: /// `float(\"-inf\")` writes the negative zero's natural sign counterpart;the conditional is what\n123: /// picks which infinity, with `a` and `b`'s signs compared rather than tested for equality so a\n124: /// signed zero divisor works.\n125: \n126: /// The `a == 0` test catching both zero signs is what makes `0.0 / 0.0` NaN,\n127: /// IEEE's answer, the same way JS's `/` gives it (kantord/toylang#149).\n128: const DIVF_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_divf(a, b):\n129:     if b == 0:\n130:         if a == 0:\n131:             return float(\"nan\")\n132:         return float(\"inf\") if (a < 0) == (b < 0) else float(\"-inf\") \n\n(Showing lines 115-132 of 946. Use offset=133 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"\"#;\n\n/// Float division spells the IEEE zero-divisor answers Python's `/` refuses to give (ADR 0007,\n/// the Q37 ruling): NaN for `0 / 0`, the infinities otherwise, with the result's sign the\n/// signs' XOR read with `<` rather than `==` because `-0.0` is a reachable value (`-0.0`\n/// is a literal)and `-0.0 < 0` tells its sign where equality cannot.\n\n/// `float(\"-inf\")` writes the negative zero's natural sign counterpart;the conditional is what\n/// picks which infinity, with `a` and `b`'s signs compared rather than tested for equality so a\n/// signed zero divisor works.\n\n/// The `a == 0` test catching both zero signs is what makes `0.0 / 0.0` NaN,\n/// IEEE's answer, the same way JS's `/` gives it (kantord/toylang#149).\nconst DIVF_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_divf(a, b):\n    if b == 0:\n        if a == 0:\n            return float(\"nan\")\n        return float(\"inf\") if (a < 0) == (b < 0) else float(\"-inf\") ","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs","text":"\"#;\n\n/// Float division spells the IEEE zero-divisor answers Python's `/` refuses to give (ADR 0007,\n/// the Q37 ruling): NaN for `0 / 0`, the infinities otherwise, with the result's sign the\n/// signs' XOR read with `<` rather than `==` because `-0.0` is a reachable value (`-0.0`\n/// is a literal)and `-0.0 < 0` tells its sign where equality cannot.\n\n/// `float(\"-inf\")` writes the negative zero's natural sign counterpart;the conditional is what\n/// picks which infinity, with `a` and `b`'s signs compared rather than tested for equality so a\n/// signed zero divisor works.\n\n/// The `a == 0` test catching both zero signs is what makes `0.0 / 0.0` NaN,\n/// IEEE's answer, the same way JS's `/` gives it (kantord/toylang#149).\nconst DIVF_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_divf(a, b):\n    if b == 0:\n        if a == 0:\n            return float(\"nan\")\n        return float(\"inf\") if (a < 0) == (b < 0) else float(\"-inf\") ","lineStart":115,"lineEnd":132,"totalLines":946,"truncated":true}},"title":"src/emit_py.rs","time":{"start":1788371143747,"end":1788371143757}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Chunk 1 succeeded! Now linesodor  ́123-125 (paragraph  ́2)and linesodor  ́127-128 (paragraph  ́3). Let me re-read the current state to get exact text.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0633a0c3b001YgtTBH41qhimmM","sessionID":"ses_f9cd2b4c5ffeW0f5SqvVxijLH6","messageID":"msg_0633a062b001uqQ8mHRtzvJu8a"}}
