lines — the call had those lines TWICE (old + new). The replace attempt had old 5 lines + new 4 lines — the call had different content. Maybe the corruption is length/content dependent. \n\nWhatever — pragmatic workaround: do the replacement as TWO steps:\n1. First no-op? — no. \n\nAlternative: replace with the new comment INCREMENTALLY: first replace a SHORT unique substring (say the first line of para2) with the new 4-line comment, then delete the rest. Let me think: replace lineodor  ́122 (`/// \\`float(\"-inf\")\\` writes...`) with my new 4-line comment + `\\n`. Then linesodor  ́123-127 remain (para2 tail, blank, para3) — delete them separately. \n\nHmm — but the deletion oldStrings would contain the same corrupt-prone chars. But maybe shorter blocks match better. \n\nActually — wait. Let me reconsider. The no-op on the 5-line block matched. So the file matches that 5-line text EXACTLY. The replace with the same oldString failed. The only difference is newString. So THE TOOL itsel (or transport) is failing to find oldString when newString is long/different?? That makes no sense... unless the edit tool applied a TRANSFORMATION to oldString as part of serializing the call (like converting `\\`` sequences...). \n\nHmm — actually — here's a hypothesis: the transport applies a transform to the entire message that, among other things, strips `)` immediately before `\\n`, inserts accents, etc. — AND this transform applies to ALL text in the message including oldString. In the no-op attempt, both oldString and newString were identical,so whatever transform hit them hit both equally — but the MATCH is against the FILE (not against newString!,so oldString-after-transform must still equal the file text. If the transform changed oldString, it wouldn't match the file unless the transform was identity on it. So in the no-op, the transform left oldString unchanged (or changed it identically... no, oldString must match file exactly after transform. So oldString passed through unchanged in the no-op. In the replace attempt, the transform changed oldString. What differs? The replace attempt's newString contained `` ` `` sequences and `(`... the no-op's newString (same as oldString) also contained them. Hmm — but maybe the transform depends on the WHOLE message, and with a longer/different newString, different parts get mangled. \n\nThis is fundamentally unpredictable. Robust strategy: make newString SHORT andSIMPLE to minimize collateral corruption. \n\nNew plan: \n1. Replace lineodor  ́122 (the first para2 line, short oldString) with my new 4-line comment (newString has backticks/parens — risk)..\n2. Delete linesodor  ́123-127 in chunks.\n\nActually — the corruption hitting oldString when newString has certain content is the core problem. To eliminate it, make newString contain NO risky chars: no backticks, no parens, no brackets. My new comment is full of backticks and parens. Ugh.\n\n.\n\n Let me instead make the new comment minimal: a single `///` line with no special chars:\n`/// Float division spells the IEEE zero-divisor answers Python's `/` refuses to give.` — backticks — remove them: `/// Float division spells the IEEE zero-divisor answers that Python's slash refuses to give.` — no risky chars? Slash ` `/` `` is fine. Parens none. Brackets none. Good.\n\nBut I'd lose the rationale (signed zero, NaN). Hmm — acceptable? The code `tl_divf` is short and self-explanatory-ish; the subtle bits (signed zero, `0/0` NaN) are worth one comment line. Let me make a TWO-line comment with minimal risky chars:\n```\n/// Float division spells the IEEE zero-divisor answers Python's `/` refuses to give: NaN\n/// for zero over zero, the infinities otherwise, signs XORed. `-0.0` is reachable,so\n/// the zero tests compare `< 0` rather than `== 0`.\n```\nStill backticks. Remove backticks:\n```\n/// Float division spells the IEEE zero-divisor answers Python's slash refuses to give: NaN\n/// for zero over zero, the infinities otherwise, signs XORed. Negative zero is reachable,so\n/// the zero tests compare with < rather than ==.\n```\nNo parens, no brackets, no backticks — only `<`/`==` (less-than signs fine?;`<` in comments fine). And `Python's slash` — apostrophe fine.\n\n Hmm — but this loses precision (\"slash\" vs `/`.\" Acceptable — or use `division` operator... let me write:\n```\n/// Float division spells the IEEE zero-divisor answers Python refuses to give: NaN for\n/// zero over zero, the infinities otherwise, signs XORed. Negative zero is reachable,so\n/// the zero tests use < not == to read a sign.\n```\nNo risky chars at all (no parens/brackets/backticks). Three clean lines. \n\nNow the replace: oldString = lineodor  ́122 (single line, risky chars (backtick/paren/apostrophe) but short. newString = the 3 clean lines. Hmm — but oldString has `float(\"-inf\")` — parens and quotes. The no-op on that line matched earlier (I tested `float(\"-inf\")\\` writes the negative zero's natural sign counterpart;` matched. So a no-op on lineodor  ́122 with that content succeeded. So oldString=that line is matchable. Let me do the replace with newString=the 3-line clean comment. If the tool corrupts oldString due to newString... newString is clean,so hopefully no collateral damage. Try.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0633b6545001jJ3NKpQuentvHC","sessionID":"ses_f9cd2b4c5ffeW0f5SqvVxijLH6","messageID":"msg_0633b1528001AU0JpgNai8zFsW"}}
