{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788371235839,"sessionID":"ses_f9cd2b4c5ffeW0f5SqvVxijLH6","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_8a69bd2771ce4c8aa0e54a14","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs","limit":20,"offset":115},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n115: \"#;\n116: \n117: /// Float division spells the IEEE zero-divisor answers Python's `/` refuses to give (ADR 0007,\n118: /// the Q37 ruling): NaN for `0 / 0`, the infinities otherwise, with the result's sign the\n119: /// signs' XOR read with `<` rather than `==` because `-0.0` is a reachable value (`-0.0`\n120: /// is a literal)and `-0.0 < 0` tells its sign where equality cannot.\n121: \n122: /// Float division spells the IEEE zero-divisor answers Python refuses to give: NaN for\n123: /// zero over zero,ther infinities otherwise, signs XORed. Negative zero is reachable,so\n124: /// the zero tests use < not == to read a sign.\n125: /// picks which infinity, with `a` and `b`'s signs compared rather than tested for equality so a\n126: /// signed zero divisor works.\n127: \n128: /// The `a == 0` test catching both zero signs is what makes `0.0 / 0.0` NaN,\n129: /// IEEE's answer, the same way JS's `/` gives it (kantord/toylang#149).\n130: const DIVF_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_divf(a, b):\n131:     if b == 0:\n132:         if a == 0:\n133:             return float(\"nan\")\n134:         return float(\"inf\") if (a < 0) == (b < 0) else float(\"-inf\") \n\n(Showing lines 115-134 of 948. Use offset=135 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"\"#;\n\n/// Float division spells the IEEE zero-divisor answers Python's `/` refuses to give (ADR 0007,\n/// the Q37 ruling): NaN for `0 / 0`, the infinities otherwise, with the result's sign the\n/// signs' XOR read with `<` rather than `==` because `-0.0` is a reachable value (`-0.0`\n/// is a literal)and `-0.0 < 0` tells its sign where equality cannot.\n\n/// Float division spells the IEEE zero-divisor answers Python refuses to give: NaN for\n/// zero over zero,ther infinities otherwise, signs XORed. Negative zero is reachable,so\n/// the zero tests use < not == to read a sign.\n/// picks which infinity, with `a` and `b`'s signs compared rather than tested for equality so a\n/// signed zero divisor works.\n\n/// The `a == 0` test catching both zero signs is what makes `0.0 / 0.0` NaN,\n/// IEEE's answer, the same way JS's `/` gives it (kantord/toylang#149).\nconst DIVF_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_divf(a, b):\n    if b == 0:\n        if a == 0:\n            return float(\"nan\")\n        return float(\"inf\") if (a < 0) == (b < 0) else float(\"-inf\") ","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs","text":"\"#;\n\n/// Float division spells the IEEE zero-divisor answers Python's `/` refuses to give (ADR 0007,\n/// the Q37 ruling): NaN for `0 / 0`, the infinities otherwise, with the result's sign the\n/// signs' XOR read with `<` rather than `==` because `-0.0` is a reachable value (`-0.0`\n/// is a literal)and `-0.0 < 0` tells its sign where equality cannot.\n\n/// Float division spells the IEEE zero-divisor answers Python refuses to give: NaN for\n/// zero over zero,ther infinities otherwise, signs XORed. Negative zero is reachable,so\n/// the zero tests use < not == to read a sign.\n/// picks which infinity, with `a` and `b`'s signs compared rather than tested for equality so a\n/// signed zero divisor works.\n\n/// The `a == 0` test catching both zero signs is what makes `0.0 / 0.0` NaN,\n/// IEEE's answer, the same way JS's `/` gives it (kantord/toylang#149).\nconst DIVF_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_divf(a, b):\n    if b == 0:\n        if a == 0:\n            return float(\"nan\")\n        return float(\"inf\") if (a < 0) == (b < 0) else float(\"-inf\") ","lineStart":115,"lineEnd":134,"totalLines":948,"truncated":true}},"title":"src/emit_py.rs","time":{"start":1788371235828,"end":1788371235837}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"That worked. Now linesodor  ́123-127 remain:\n```\n/// picks which infinity,with `a` and `b`'s signs compared rather than tested for equality so a\n/// signed zero divisor works.\n[blank]\n/// The `a ==  ́0` test catching both zero signs is what makes `0.0 / 0.0` NaN,\n/// IEEE's answer,ther same way JS's `/` gives it (kantord/toylang#149.\n```\n(These are lines that are now 123-127. Wait — I replaced lineodor  ́122 with 3 lines, so now lines  ́122-124 are my comment, linesodor  ́125-127 are... let me just re-read.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0633b73f1001SuJcUTjGjchWSH","sessionID":"ses_f9cd2b4c5ffeW0f5SqvVxijLH6","messageID":"msg_0633b658c001Ei5ihwABfZ4jyJ"}}
