t block is currently messy (two versions of the comment, stray para2/para3 leftovers).. I could leave it messy? No — it would be broken text (duplicated comments, stray sentences).\n\nAlternatively — use the `edit` tool's replaceAll? No.\n\n\n\nOK — let me try the python-script approach for cleaning up this one block. I'll write a script that:\n1. Reads src/emit_py.rs.\n\n2. Finds the span from the end of FLOAT_HELPER (`\"#;` + newline) to the start of `const DIVF_HELPER` line.\n3. Replaces that span with a clean comment block (typed by me, kept simple).\n4. Writes back.\n\n\n\nThe script content I type — keep it as simple as possible, with trailing-space convention. The replacement comment — keep it short, and I'll verify it after. Let me author it.\n\n.\n\nActually — even simpler: replace the whole span between the two `\"#;` markers... hmm, FLOAT_HELPER ends at `\"#;` (lineodor  ́115)and DIVF_HELPER starts at `const DIVF_HELPER` (lineodor  ́126). The span between is linesodor  ́116-125 (blank + comment blocks).. I'll replace lines from after `\"#;` through before `const DIVF_HELPER` with: `\\n/// <comment>\\n`. \n\nScript:\n```python\ntext = open('src/emit_py.rs').read()\na = text.index('const DIVF_HELPER')\nb = text.rindex('\"#;', 0, a) + 4\ncomment = '\\n/// Float division spells the IEEE zero-divisor answers Python refuses to give: NaN\\n/// for zero over zero, the infinities otherwise, signs XORed. Negative zero is reachable,so\\n/// the zero tests use < not == to read a sign.\\n'\ntext = text[:b] + comment + text[a:]\nopen('src/emit_py.rs', 'w').write(text)\n```\nWait — I want to keep ONE blank line between `\"#;` and the comment, and ONE between comment and const. `\"#;` is followed by `\\n` already;so after `\"#;` newline, then comment lines, then `\\n`, then const. Hmm — `comment` starts with `\\n`? Let me set comment = `\\n/// Float...\\n/// ...\\n/// ...\\n\\n` — ends with blank line before const. So: text[:b] (ends after `\"#;`) + `\\n` + `/// ...` lines + `\\n\\n` + `const...`. Actually simpler: comment = `\\n/// Float division spells the IEEE zero-divisor answers Python refuses to give: NaN\\n/// for zero over zero, the infinities otherwise, signs XORed. Negative zero is reachable,so\\n/// the zero tests use < not == to read a sign.\\n\\n`;then text[:b]+comment+text[a:]. \n\nb = index after `\"#;` — `text.rindex('\"#;', 0, a) + 4` — `\"#;` is 4 chars (`\"`, `#`, `;` — wait, `\"#;` is `\"` `#` `;` — 3 chars? `\"#;` — chars: `\"`,`#`,`;` — 3 chars. `rindex` returns index of start;+3 gives position after the 3 chars. Then the next char in text is `\\n`(the newline ending lineodor  ́115). So after `b`, text continues with `\\n` then the junk. My comment starts with `\\n`? If comment = `\\n/// Float...` — then text[:b] + comment — gives `\"#;` + `\\n` + `/// Float...`. Good. The junk (old blank+comments) — I cut it out by using text[a:] where a = start of `const DIVF_HELPER`. So everything between `\"#;`+`\\n` and `const DIVF_HELPER` is removed. The comment ends with `\\n\\n` then `const...`. Good — one blank line between comment and const. \n\nLet me author this script with the write tool, using trailing-space convention on any line ending with `)`. The script has parens mid-line (fine. Let me write it carefully.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0633c1326001E6HxWzikAYC1xn","sessionID":"ses_f9cd2b4c5ffeW0f5SqvVxijLH6","messageID":"msg_0633bd38f001t1rUyoJe3we2yL"}}
