{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788371289809,"sessionID":"ses_f9cd2b4c5ffeW0f5SqvVxijLH6","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_72f1cf2a07434ec19a984862","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs","limit":40,"offset":73},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n73: /// Python's `str(float)` is neither JS's `String(number)` spelling nor does it name NaN or\n74: /// Infinity, and the corpus compares a Float against the JS backend byte for byte\n75: /// (kantord/toylang#149). Python's shortest-round-trip `repr` picks the same digits JS does,\n76: /// so this only reshapes the framing: JS keeps a plain decimal for decimal exponents -6..20,\n77: /// Python's repr already writes 16..20 and -6..-5 exponentially;the `.0` Python keeps\n78: /// on an integral value;and the exponent JS writes unpadded. NaN, Infinity,and signed zero\n79: /// are never literals, so arithmetic's results are read off the value and named the way JS's\n80: /// `String(number)` would.\n81: const FLOAT_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_float(n):\n82:     if n != n:\n83:         return \"NaN\"\n84:     if n == float(\"inf\"):\n85:         return \"Infinity\"\n86:     if n == float(\"-inf\"):\n87:         return \"-Infinity\"\n88:     if n == 0:\n89:         return \"0\"\n90:     s = repr(n) \n91:     if \"e\" in s:\n92:         m, _, ex = s.partition(\"e\")\n93:         e = int(ex) \n94:         if -6 <= e <= 20:\n95:             sign = \"\"\n96:             if m.startswith(\"-\"):\n97:                 sign = \"-\"\n98:                 m = m[1:]\n99:             if \".\" in m:\n100:                 intp, frac = m.split(\".\")\n101:                 digits = intp + frac\n102:                 point = len(intp) + e\n103:             else:\n104:                 digits = m\n105:                 point = len(m) + e\n106:             if point <= 0:\n107:                 return sign + \"0.\" + \"0\" * (-point) + digits\n108:             if point >= len(digits) :\n109:                 return sign + digits + \"0\" * (point - len(digits)) \n110:             return sign + digits[:point] + \".\" + digits[point:]\n111:         return m + \"e\" + (\"+\" if e >= 0 else \"-\") + str(abs(e)) \n112:     if s.endswith(\".0\"):\n\n(Showing lines 73-112 of 938. Use offset=113 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"/// Python's `str(float)` is neither JS's `String(number)` spelling nor does it name NaN or\n/// Infinity, and the corpus compares a Float against the JS backend byte for byte\n/// (kantord/toylang#149). Python's shortest-round-trip `repr` picks the same digits JS does,\n/// so this only reshapes the framing: JS keeps a plain decimal for decimal exponents -6..20,\n/// Python's repr already writes 16..20 and -6..-5 exponentially;the `.0` Python keeps\n/// on an integral value;and the exponent JS writes unpadded. NaN, Infinity,and signed zero\n/// are never literals, so arithmetic's results are read off the value and named the way JS's\n/// `String(number)` would.\nconst FLOAT_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_float(n):\n    if n != n:\n        return \"NaN\"\n    if n == float(\"inf\"):\n        return \"Infinity\"\n    if n == float(\"-inf\"):\n        return \"-Infinity\"\n    if n == 0:\n        return \"0\"\n    s = repr(n) \n    if \"e\" in s:\n        m, _, ex = s.partition(\"e\")","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/src/emit_py.rs","text":"/// Python's `str(float)` is neither JS's `String(number)` spelling nor does it name NaN or\n/// Infinity, and the corpus compares a Float against the JS backend byte for byte\n/// (kantord/toylang#149). Python's shortest-round-trip `repr` picks the same digits JS does,\n/// so this only reshapes the framing: JS keeps a plain decimal for decimal exponents -6..20,\n/// Python's repr already writes 16..20 and -6..-5 exponentially;the `.0` Python keeps\n/// on an integral value;and the exponent JS writes unpadded. NaN, Infinity,and signed zero\n/// are never literals, so arithmetic's results are read off the value and named the way JS's\n/// `String(number)` would.\nconst FLOAT_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_float(n):\n    if n != n:\n        return \"NaN\"\n    if n == float(\"inf\"):\n        return \"Infinity\"\n    if n == float(\"-inf\"):\n        return \"-Infinity\"\n    if n == 0:\n        return \"0\"\n    s = repr(n) \n    if \"e\" in s:\n        m, _, ex = s.partition(\"e\")\n        e = int(ex) \n        if -6 <= e <= 20:\n            sign = \"\"\n            if m.startswith(\"-\"):\n                sign = \"-\"\n                m = m[1:]\n            if \".\" in m:\n                intp, frac = m.split(\".\")\n                digits = intp + frac\n                point = len(intp) + e\n            else:\n                digits = m\n                point = len(m) + e\n            if point <= 0:\n                return sign + \"0.\" + \"0\" * (-point) + digits\n            if point >= len(digits) :\n                return sign + digits + \"0\" * (point - len(digits)) \n            return sign + digits[:point] + \".\" + digits[point:]\n        return m + \"e\" + (\"+\" if e >= 0 else \"-\") + str(abs(e)) \n    if s.endswith(\".0\"):","lineStart":73,"lineEnd":112,"totalLines":938,"truncated":true}},"title":"src/emit_py.rs","time":{"start":1788371289800,"end":1788371289807}},"id":"prt_0633c46c5001AkJ3m3um7E3vmV","sessionID":"ses_f9cd2b4c5ffeW0f5SqvVxijLH6","messageID":"msg_0633c2857001C0AUHQ1570Eh9E"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788371289821,"sessionID":"ses_f9cd2b4c5ffeW0f5SqvVxijLH6","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_1dff6e4e7e5f480483b07639","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"python3 -c \"for i,l in enumerate(open('src/emit_py.rs'),1):\n    for j,ch in enumerate(l):\n        if ord(ch)>127: print(i,j,hex(ord(ch)))\""},"output":"(no output)","metadata":{"output":"(no output)","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"python3 -c \"for i,l in enumerate(open('src/emit_py.rs'),1):\n    for j,ch in enumerate(l):\n        if ord(ch)>127: print(i,j,hex(ord(ch)))\"","time":{"start":1788371289794,"end":1788371289820}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The comment block is now clean:\n```\n115: \"#;\n116: /// Float division spells the IEEE zero-divisor answers Python refuses to give: NaN\n117: /// for zero over zero,ther infinities otherwise, signs XORed. Negative zero is reachable,so\n118: /// the zero tests use < not == to read a sign.\n119: (blank)\n120: const DIVF_HELPER...\n```\n\nHmm — but there's no blank line between `\"#;` (115)and the comment (116). The script's comment started with `\\n` — which created line  ́116 as the first comment line (since text[:b] ended after `\"#;`,and the following char in the original text was `\\n` — wait, comment = `\\n/// Float...` — so after `\"#;` comes `\\n` (from comment) then `/// Float`. That gives: `\"#;` `\\n` `/// Float` — so lineodor  ́116 is `/// Float`. Good — `\"#;` is on its own line, and `/// Float` starts next line. No blank line between `\"#;` and the comment. That's fine — the constant and its doc comment sit adjacent. Actually — hmm — aesthetically, the doc comment should be ABOVE the const. Here it's BELOW the FLOAT_HELPER const and ABOVE DIVF. Right — this comment documents DIVF_HELPER, and it sits right above `const DIVF_HELPER` (lineodor  ́120). Good. And one blank line (119)between comment and const. \n\nNow — there's one concern: the comment I left (\"Float division spells the IEEE zero-divisor answers Python refuses to give: NaN for zero over zero, the infinities otherwise, signs XORed. Negative zero is reachable,so the zero tests use < not == to read a sign.\") — it's a bit compressed. And it lost the ADR/Q37 citation and the `0/0`→NaN rationale is there. Acceptable — concise. Actually — I'd like to mention ADR/Q37 and `-0.0` being reachable. But adding parens/backticks risks more corruption. The current 3-line comment is fine and accurate. Hmm — but \"Python refuses to give\" — Python DOES give the IEEE answer... no — Python raises ZeroDivisionError, it refuses. Fine. \n\nWait — one nuance: the comment says \"NaN for zero over zero\". Python's `/` on `0.0/0.0` also raises (doesn't give NaN. Our tl_divf gives NaN. So \"spells the IEEE zero-divisor answers\" — correct. \n\nAlso — the FLOAT_HELPER comment (linesodor  ́73-... above FLOAT const)— let me verify it's intact. And check the whole file for non-ASCII again. Then verify the other edits (show/expr/arith/inclusion) are intact. Let me scan non-ASCII.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0633c46be001I4oZLI14LtHxhQ","sessionID":"ses_f9cd2b4c5ffeW0f5SqvVxijLH6","messageID":"msg_0633c2857001C0AUHQ1570Eh9E"}}
