{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788371304076,"sessionID":"ses_f9cd2b4c5ffeW0f5SqvVxijLH6","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_60bf06320d4b464b8d7c6b61","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/tests/backend_js.rs"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/tests/backend_js.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: //! What the corpus cannot express. Behaviour that must be identical across backends lives in\n2: //! tests/corpus/ and is checked by tests/corpus.rs; this file holds the claims that need more\n3: //! than one run of one program to state.\n4: \n5: use toylang::Backend;\n6: \n7: #[track_caller]\n8: fn agree(src: &str, stdin: Option<&str>) -> String {\n9:     let mut results = Backend::ALL\n10:         .iter()\n11:         .map(|b| (b.name(), toylang::run_on(src, stdin, *b).unwrap()));\n12:     let (first_name, first) = results.next().expect(\"at least one backend\");\n13:     for (name, out) in results {\n14:         assert_eq!(first, out, \"{first_name} and {name} disagree on:\\n{src}\");\n15:     }\n16:     first\n17: }\n18: \n19: /// Record keys come out in the type's order on every backend, not in the order the input\n20: /// happened to list them. Two inputs, one expected output, so a backend reverting to insertion\n21: /// order fails here. A corpus entry has one input and cannot say this.\n22: #[test]\n23: fn record_key_order_follows_the_type() {\n24:     let src = r#\"\n25: fn first(db: {u: {name: Str, age: Int}}) -> {name: Str, age: Int} = db.u\n26: \n27: first(input)\n28: \"#;\n29:     let declared_order = agree(src, Some(r#\"{\"u\": {\"name\": \"ada\", \"age\": 36}}\"#));\n30:     let reversed = agree(src, Some(r#\"{\"u\": {\"age\": 36, \"name\": \"ada\"}}\"#));\n31:     assert_eq!(declared_order, reversed);\n32:     insta::assert_snapshot!(declared_order);\n33: }\n34: \n35: // Float is JS-only in this row (kantord/toylang#149): the other backends do not carry it yet,\n36: // so none of these can be a corpus case, which would require every backend to agree. They live\n37: // here, JS-only, until the follow-up rows bring Float to the rest of the backends.\n38: \n39: /// A decimal-point literal parses, type-checks, and prints through the JS printer.\n40: #[test]\n41: fn float_literals_parse_and_print() {\n42:     let out = toylang::run_on(\"1.5 + 0.25 * 2.0\\n\", None, Backend::Js).unwrap();\n43:     assert_eq!(out, \"2\\n\");\n44: }\n45: \n46: /// Division on a Float is total (ADR 0007, the Q37 ruling): a zero divisor is the IEEE answer,\n47: /// Infinity, not the stop an Int's `1 / 0` is.\n48: #[test]\n49: fn float_division_by_zero_is_infinity() {\n50:     let out = toylang::run_on(\"1.0 / 0.0\\n\", None, Backend::Js).unwrap();\n51:     assert_eq!(out, \"Infinity\\n\");\n52: }\n53: \n54: /// NaN and Infinity are values a Float can hold, and both print as their names.\n55: #[test]\n56: fn float_nan_and_infinity_are_producible() {\n57:     let nan = toylang::run_on(\"0.0 / 0.0\\n\", None, Backend::Js).unwrap();\n58:     assert_eq!(nan, \"NaN\\n\");\n59:     let neg = toylang::run_on(\"-1.0 / 0.0\\n\", None, Backend::Js).unwrap();\n60:     assert_eq!(neg, \"-Infinity\\n\");\n61: }\n62: \n63: /// Float comparisons type-check and print a Bool.\n64: #[test]\n65: fn float_comparison() {\n66:     let out = toylang::run_on(\"1.5 < 2.5\\n\", None, Backend::Js).unwrap();\n67:     assert_eq!(out, \"true\\n\");\n68: }\n69: \n70: /// A Float result keeps its value when read back off the wire: a JSON number already is the\n71: /// double a Float names (ADR 0007), the case that made Int64 refuse `input`.\n72: #[test]\n73: fn float_input_reads_a_json_number() {\n74:     let src = \"fn twice(x: Float) -> Float = x * 2.0\\n\\ntwice(input)\\n\";\n75:     let out = toylang::run_on(src, Some(\"2.5\"), Backend::Js).unwrap();\n76:     assert_eq!(out, \"5\\n\");\n77: }\n\n(End of file - total 77 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"//! What the corpus cannot express. Behaviour that must be identical across backends lives in\n//! tests/corpus/ and is checked by tests/corpus.rs; this file holds the claims that need more\n//! than one run of one program to state.\n\nuse toylang::Backend;\n\n#[track_caller]\nfn agree(src: &str, stdin: Option<&str>) -> String {\n    let mut results = Backend::ALL\n        .iter()\n        .map(|b| (b.name(), toylang::run_on(src, stdin, *b).unwrap()));\n    let (first_name, first) = results.next().expect(\"at least one backend\");\n    for (name, out) in results {\n        assert_eq!(first, out, \"{first_name} and {name} disagree on:\\n{src}\");\n    }\n    first\n}\n\n/// Record keys come out in the type's order on every backend, not in the order the input\n/// happened to list them. Two inputs, one expected output, so a backend reverting to insertion","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-python/tests/backend_js.rs","text":"//! What the corpus cannot express. Behaviour that must be identical across backends lives in\n//! tests/corpus/ and is checked by tests/corpus.rs; this file holds the claims that need more\n//! than one run of one program to state.\n\nuse toylang::Backend;\n\n#[track_caller]\nfn agree(src: &str, stdin: Option<&str>) -> String {\n    let mut results = Backend::ALL\n        .iter()\n        .map(|b| (b.name(), toylang::run_on(src, stdin, *b).unwrap()));\n    let (first_name, first) = results.next().expect(\"at least one backend\");\n    for (name, out) in results {\n        assert_eq!(first, out, \"{first_name} and {name} disagree on:\\n{src}\");\n    }\n    first\n}\n\n/// Record keys come out in the type's order on every backend, not in the order the input\n/// happened to list them. Two inputs, one expected output, so a backend reverting to insertion\n/// order fails here. A corpus entry has one input and cannot say this.\n#[test]\nfn record_key_order_follows_the_type() {\n    let src = r#\"\nfn first(db: {u: {name: Str, age: Int}}) -> {name: Str, age: Int} = db.u\n\nfirst(input)\n\"#;\n    let declared_order = agree(src, Some(r#\"{\"u\": {\"name\": \"ada\", \"age\": 36}}\"#));\n    let reversed = agree(src, Some(r#\"{\"u\": {\"age\": 36, \"name\": \"ada\"}}\"#));\n    assert_eq!(declared_order, reversed);\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(declared_order);\n}\n\n// Float is JS-only in this row (kantord/toylang#149): the other backends do not carry it yet,\n// so none of these can be a corpus case, which would require every backend to agree. They live\n// here, JS-only, until the follow-up rows bring Float to the rest of the backends.\n\n/// A decimal-point literal parses, type-checks, and prints through the JS printer.\n#[test]\nfn float_literals_parse_and_print() {\n    let out = toylang::run_on(\"1.5 + 0.25 * 2.0\\n\", None, Backend::Js).unwrap();\n    assert_eq!(out, \"2\\n\");\n}\n\n/// Division on a Float is total (ADR 0007, the Q37 ruling): a zero divisor is the IEEE answer,\n/// Infinity, not the stop an Int's `1 / 0` is.\n#[test]\nfn float_division_by_zero_is_infinity() {\n    let out = toylang::run_on(\"1.0 / 0.0\\n\", None, Backend::Js).unwrap();\n    assert_eq!(out, \"Infinity\\n\");\n}\n\n/// NaN and Infinity are values a Float can hold, and both print as their names.\n#[test]\nfn float_nan_and_infinity_are_producible() {\n    let nan = toylang::run_on(\"0.0 / 0.0\\n\", None, Backend::Js).unwrap();\n    assert_eq!(nan, \"NaN\\n\");\n    let neg = toylang::run_on(\"-1.0 / 0.0\\n\", None, Backend::Js).unwrap();\n    assert_eq!(neg, \"-Infinity\\n\");\n}\n\n/// Float comparisons type-check and print a Bool.\n#[test]\nfn float_comparison() {\n    let out = toylang::run_on(\"1.5 < 2.5\\n\", None, Backend::Js).unwrap();\n    assert_eq!(out, \"true\\n\");\n}\n\n/// A Float result keeps its value when read back off the wire: a JSON number already is the\n/// double a Float names (ADR 0007), the case that made Int64 refuse `input`.\n#[test]\nfn float_input_reads_a_json_number() {\n    let src = \"fn twice(x: Float) -> Float = x * 2.0\\n\\ntwice(input)\\n\";\n    let out = toylang::run_on(src, Some(\"2.5\"), Backend::Js).unwrap();\n    assert_eq!(out, \"5\\n\");\n}","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":77,"totalLines":77,"truncated":false}},"title":"tests/backend_js.rs","time":{"start":1788371304065,"end":1788371304073}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"So a Float literal needs either a fraction (`.digits`) or an exponent (`e...`) — so `1.5`, `2.0`, `1e16` (exponent only, is_float=true → Float!), `1.0e16` all work. `1e16` IS a Float literal (Tok::Float). Great — so I can test the exponent-boundary formatting directly: `1e16` as a program printing a Float → should print `\"10000000000000000\"` (JS-style plain form. And `1e-5` → `\"0.00001\"`. And `1e21` → `\"1e+21\"`. And `1e-7` → `\"1e-7\"`. \n\nThese are excellent Python-specific test cases (they'd catch tl_float's reshaping bugs). Let me add them to backend_py.rs.\n\nNow, let me look at the JS test file's exact structure to mirror the style, and check how tests call `run_on` (Backend::Py etc.).","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0633c7e7a001yS4yX0HtqOkLUA","sessionID":"ses_f9cd2b4c5ffeW0f5SqvVxijLH6","messageID":"msg_0633c735c001vUbsjanF7a29N1"}}
